/** @page release_notes Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @section qore_3_0_0 Qore 3.0.0

    @par Release Summary
    @parblock
    %Qore 3.0.0 is a major release whose central themes are:
    - <b>Faster execution and reduced memory footprint</b>
    - <b>Scalability</b> for high-concurrency workloads
    - <b>Code sandboxing and execution control</b>
    - <b>Greatly expanded integration capabilities and protocol support</b>
    - <b>Fast text processing</b>
    - <b>Foundations for AI and ML workloads</b>

    <b>Highlights</b> (the sections below summarize each change and link to its documentation):

    <i>Performance and memory:</i>
    - <b><a href="https://llvm.org/">LLVM</a>-backed @ref execution_modes "execution modes"</b>: an IR interpreter,
      JIT, @ref exec_mode_tiered "tiered" execution (the default for @ref modern "%modern" code) and
      @ref exec_mode_aot "AOT" compilation with the new @ref qcc_tool "qcc" compiler; standard modules are installed
      AOT-compiled
    - values take 8 bytes instead of 16 (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NaN-boxing">NaN-boxing</a>), with
      short strings stored inline, @ref string_views "string views" and dense @ref buffer "buffer<T>" values

    <i>Language:</i>
    - @ref modern "%modern" is the default for new code (<tt>.qr</tt> files and all files other than <tt>.q</tt>)
    - @ref generic_classes "generic classes", @ref hashdecl_inheritance "hashdecl inheritance",
      @ref enums "enums", @ref typedef_keyword "typedef", @ref union_type "union types" and typed
      @ref code_complex_type "callables"
    - @ref function_named_arguments "named arguments", @ref streaming_first "streaming operators" and
      @ref char "char" values
    - targeted parse diagnostics and runtime hints for @ref common_pitfalls "common pitfalls"

    <i>Concurrency:</i>
    - @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future"/@ref Qore::Thread::Promise "Promise", @ref Qore::Thread::Channel "Channel",
      @ref Qore::Thread::WaitGroup "WaitGroup", @ref Qore::Thread::Semaphore "Semaphore" and
      @ref Qore::parallel_map() "parallel_map()"
    - cooperative thread cancellation with @ref Qore::cancel_thread() "cancel_thread()"

    <i>Sandboxing and execution control:</i>
    - @ref sandboxing "sandboxing" with @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager": filesystem, network
      (including SSRF prevention), thread, wall-time, CPU-time, stack and memory limits and interrupt controls

    <i>Networking and I/O:</i>
    - all I/O runs through the asynchronous I/O controller (@ref Qore::AsyncIoController "AsyncIoController"), so a
      thread can serve many connections
    - <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9113">HTTP/2</a> and
      <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9114">HTTP/3</a> in @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" and the
      servers, WebSockets and Server-Sent Events over every HTTP version, and Happy Eyeballs connection racing
    - new asynchronous client and server modules: @ref httpclientiointro "HttpClientIo",
      @ref ftpclientiointro "FtpClientIo", @ref httpserverasynciointro "HttpServerAsyncIo" and
      @ref asyncsocketiointro "AsyncSocketIo"

    <i>Data, SQL and analytics:</i>
    - typed and columnar SQL results (@ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult "ColumnarResult") and the
      @ref dataframeintro "dataframe" module, with <a href="https://arrow.apache.org/">Arrow</a> interchange
    - @ref dataprovider_dpql "DPQL" queries and columnar bulk operations in the data provider modules

    <i>Text processing and regular expressions:</i>
    - @ref fast_text_processing "fast text processing" APIs (@ref Qore::Scanner "Scanner", lazy string iterators and
      splitters)
    - precompiled regular expressions (@ref Qore::Regex "Regex"), named captures,
      @ref qore_regex_brace_delimiters "brace delimiters" and Unicode-aware case conversion

    <i>Modules and integrations:</i>
    - new builtin modules, including @ref avrointro "avro", @ref dataframeintro "dataframe", @ref mlintro "ml",
      @ref mongodbintro "mongodb" and @ref protobufintro "protobuf"; @ref jsonintro "json",
      @ref linenoiseintro "linenoise" and @ref yamlintro "yaml" are now part of %Qore
      (see @ref qore_3_0_0_new_builtin_modules "New Builtin Modules")
    - hundreds of new user modules for AI/LLM, ML, cloud, e-commerce, CRM, ERP and observability services
      (see @ref qore_3_0_0_new_user_modules "New User Modules")
    - many updated modules (see @ref qore_3_0_0_updated_modules "Updated Modules")

    <i>Tooling:</i>
    - a REPL in the \c qore binary (@ref qorereplintro "QoreRepl"), @ref prepend-module-path "per-program module paths"
      and @ref try-child-module "child modules"
    @endparblock

    @subsection qore_3_0_0_compatibility Changes That Can Affect Backwards-Compatibility

    @par No-Op Builtin Variants Are Not Called at Run Time With Strict Arguments
    With @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_TYPES or @ref Qore::PO_STRICT_ARGS (as with @ref modern "%modern"), the builtin variants
    flagged @ref NOOP are no longer resolved at run time, as at parse time: <tt>sort(NOTHING)</tt>, for example with
    an unassigned member, and the other no-op calls raise \c RUNTIME-OVERLOAD-ERROR instead of returning a constant
    value; <tt>length()</tt> and <tt>strlen()</tt> of @ref nothing keep returning \c 0 (regular variants now).

    @par Date Literals Have the Type of Their Subtype
    A date literal or constant is typed as \c date&lt;absolute&gt; or \c date&lt;relative&gt;, so one given where the
    other subtype is required is a parse error instead of a \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR; see
    @ref date_literal_subtypes.

    @par Call Stack Frames Are Located at Their Calls
    Each frame that get_thread_call_stack() and exception call stacks give is located at its call expression, with
    its columns in new \c start_column and \c end_column keys, in every execution mode, so a call in a statement of
    more than one line gives the call's lines instead of the statement's; see @ref Qore::CallStackInfo "CallStackInfo".

    @par Accepted Sockets Do Not Resolve the Peer's Hostname by Default
    Socket::accept() and Socket::acceptSSL() no longer look up the peer's hostname, so the accepted Socket's
    \c source_host member is @ref nothing unless the listening socket was set with Socket::setSourceHostLookup(); the
    lookup delayed every accept, and \c source (the peer's IP address) is set as before.  Socket::getPeerInfo()
    still resolves the hostname on request.

    @par Restricted Code Cannot Change a Module's State
    The code of a sandboxed %Program, or of a %Program with \c PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL set, can read but no longer
    change the public variables of a trusted module or the static members of its classes, which every importer
    shares: a change raises \c MODULE-VARIABLE-READ-ONLY, and such code must call a function or method that the
    module exports to set the value; see @ref module_public_variables.

    @par Modules May Not Declare Public thread_local Variables
    A module that declares a <tt>public thread_local</tt> variable raises \c MODULE-PUBLIC-THREAD-LOCAL when parsed or
    compiled; keep the variable private and export functions that read and set it (see @ref thread_local).  A module
    compiled by an earlier \c qcc that exports one is refused when loaded and must be changed and recompiled.  A
    %Program's <tt>public thread_local</tt> variables are still passed to its child Programs.

    @par The Descriptor of a Socket Is Not Available to Qore Code
    \c Socket::getSocket() is removed and @ref Qore::Socket::getPollableDescriptor() "Socket::getPollableDescriptor()"
    raises \c SOCKET-DESCRIPTOR-ERROR: every operation on a socket is made by the async I/O controller, which owns
    its non-blocking descriptor; a poll operation that waits for a socket gives the socket itself
    (@ref Qore::ExtraPollFdInfo "ExtraPollFdInfo" \c pollable).

    @par Destructors Cannot Assign Thread-Local Variables While a Worker Thread Releases Its Values
    A worker thread of a @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" or of the
    @ref Qore::AsyncIoController "AsyncIoController" releases the values of its @ref thread_local variables between
    its tasks; as at the end of a thread, the destructors that the release runs now cannot assign
    <tt><b>thread_local</b></tt> variables (\c DESTRUCTOR-ERROR): a destructor giving a variable a value whose
    destructor gave it a value again kept the worker releasing values for ever (see @ref thread_local_lifetime)

    @par Final Methods Hold for the Whole Class Hierarchy
    A subclass can no longer bypass a @ref final_methods "final" method with an overload that accepts the same
    arguments or with a parent class listed before the final method's class that declares or inherits its name;
    both are now parse errors.

    @par Module Code Runs in the Module's Program
    User code called from another %Program, such as a module function, a method of a module class or a function
    imported with @ref Qore::Program::importFunction() "Program::importFunction()", runs in the %Program that defined
    it with that %Program's names and options, governed by the caller's @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager"
    and, for module code, by a sandboxed caller's restrictions (see @ref user_module_runtime_context).

    @par A List of Selectors Removes Nothing From a Value That Is Not a List, a String or a Binary
    @ref remove "remove" and @ref delete "delete" with a list of selectors in square brackets on a hash, a number, an
    object or @ref nothing (ex: <tt>remove h[0, 1]</tt>) evaluate the selectors and remove nothing in every execution
    mode; the AST interpreter removed the whole value without evaluating them (see @ref remove_selector_list).

    @par Temporary Values Live Until the End of Their Statement
    A temporary value, such as an object returned by a call in a chain of member accesses and method calls, the operand
    of an operator or a value read through a reference, lives until the end of its statement; given to a call as an
    argument, it is released when the call returns.  Code that relies on earlier destruction must assign the value to
    a variable; see @ref temporary_lifetime.

    @par Data Provider Types Restored From Their Descriptions Can Be Cyclic and Shared
    \c DataProvider::AbstractDataProviderType::get() restores each type of a description once, so a recursive type is
    restored as a cycle and a shared type as one type; code that walks the fields of restored types must recognize
    the types it has already visited.  See @ref dataprovider_v3_11.

    @par Data Provider Types Refuse Values With TYPE-VALUE-ERROR
    A value that a data provider type refuses raises \c TYPE-VALUE-ERROR, and a refused option value
    \c OPTION-VALUE-ERROR, instead of \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR, which now means a type error in the code; soft types also
    refuse text that is no number or date.  Code that catches a refused value by its error code must use the new
    codes.  See @ref dataprovider_v3_11.

    @par date() Raises an Exception for Text After a Date or Time
    @ref Qore::date(string) "date(string)" and @ref Qore::TimeZone::date(string) "TimeZone::date(string)" raise
    \c INVALID-DATE for text after a date or time other than white space, a zone designator, \c UTC, \c GMT or the
    format in which %Qore writes dates, and read a UTC offset after a date without a time; code that passes such text
    must remove it first.  See @ref date_string_conversion.

    @par Durations Follow ISO 8601
    Duration literals and @ref Qore::date(string) "date(string)" follow the two duration formats of ISO 8601: a
    literal that does not is a parse error and a string raises \c INVALID-DATE (ex: \c P1H must be written \c PT1H),
    while weeks, a comma as the decimal sign and the alternative format are read as ISO 8601 defines them.  See
    @ref short_reldates and @ref long_reldates.

    @par The Ends of File::getPipe() Are Closed
    The ends of a pipe made by @ref Qore::File::getPipe() "File::getPipe()" own their descriptors, which
    \c close() and the destruction of the object close; keep the object open as long as its descriptor is used
    elsewhere.

    @par Writes to a Nonblocking File Wait for Room
    A write to a @ref Qore::File "File" whose descriptor is nonblocking waits for room instead of raising
    \c FILE-WRITE-ERROR (\c EAGAIN).  See @ref file_blocking_io.

    @par Child Processes Do Not Use the Database Connections of Their Parent
    A child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" gives up the database connections that it inherits and
    opens its own; continuing a transaction of the parent raises \c DATASOURCE-FORK-ERROR.  See @ref datasource_fork.

    @par Binary Modules Implement Module API 2.0
    Every external binary module must be rebuilt for module API 2.0, which registers its metadata with a
    \c {name}_qore_module_desc() function and reports initialization errors as exceptions; see "Developing Qore
    Modules" in the C++ API documentation.

    @par AsyncSocketIo 3.0 Requires an Owner for Each Operation and Has No exec() Method
    @ref AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController::submit() "AsyncSocketIoController::submit()" requires an \c owner
    in the operation info, and \c exec() is removed: wait on the Queue that \c submit() returns instead.  See
    @ref asyncsocketio_3_0.

    @par The Short Command-Line Options -M and -G Have New Meanings
    \c -M is \c --modern and \c -G is \c --enable-debug; use \c --no-namespace-defs and \c --no-global-vars for the
    restrictions they set in %Qore 2.2.  See @ref command_line_processing.

    @par Four Integer Parse Option Constants Are 0
    @ref Qore::PO_NO_CLASS_DEFS "PO_NO_CLASS_DEFS", @ref Qore::PO_NO_CONSTANT_DEFS "PO_NO_CONSTANT_DEFS",
    @ref Qore::PO_NO_NAMESPACE_DEFS "PO_NO_NAMESPACE_DEFS" and @ref Qore::PO_NO_NEW "PO_NO_NEW" are 0 and restrict
    nothing when passed as integers; set these options with @ref Qore::ParseOptions "ParseOptions", parse directives
    or command-line options (see @ref qore_3_0_0_deprecated).

    @par Thread-Local Data Is Freed With the Program That Owns It
    A thread's @ref Qore::save_thread_data() "thread-local data" is freed when the root %Program of its family is
    destroyed, also for a thread that is still alive, such as an idle pool worker.  See
    @ref thread_local_data_family.

    @par A Bare Name in a Class Refers to the Class Before a Top-Level Local Variable
    With @ref allow-bare-refs "%allow-bare-refs", a bare name in a method refers to the class's members, constants,
    static variables and methods before a top-level local variable of the same name.  See
    @ref bare_refs_top_level_locals.

    @par A Slice Is an Lvalue Only of remove and delete
    A hash or list slice used as an lvalue other than as the whole lvalue of @ref remove "remove" or
    @ref delete "delete" is a parse error, or raises \c ILLEGAL-SLICE at run time.  See @ref hash_element_operator
    and @ref list_element_operator.

    @par Stream Conversions Raise an Error for a Character the Target Encoding Cannot Hold
    @ref Qore::EncodingConversionInputStream "EncodingConversionInputStream" and
    @ref Qore::EncodingConversionOutputStream "EncodingConversionOutputStream" raise \c ENCODING-CONVERSION-ERROR
    for such a character, as @ref Qore::convert_encoding() "convert_encoding()" does, instead of transliterating it;
    the \c ICONV_TRANSLIT build option is removed (qoretechnologies/qore#5479).

    @par The Opacity of an RGB Color Is a Number From 0 to 1
    The \c a member of @ref DataProvider::RgbColorDataType "RgbColorDataType" is an opacity from \c 0 to \c 1 (default
    \c 1), returned as a \c float; a value outside of this range, such as \c 255, raises \c TYPE-VALUE-ERROR.

    @par Text in the Other Byte Order Than Its UTF-16 or UTF-32 Encoding Is Refused
    @ref Qore::StreamReader "StreamReader", @ref Qore::BufferedStreamReader "BufferedStreamReader" and
    @ref Qore::InputStreamLineIterator "InputStreamLineIterator" raise \c ENCODING-BYTE-ORDER-ERROR for text whose
    byte order mark or zero bytes show the other byte order than its encoding.  See
    @ref Qore::check_unicode_byte_order() "check_unicode_byte_order()".

    @par Fault Signals Cannot Have Qore Signal Handlers
    \c SIGBUS, \c SIGILL, \c SIGFPE and \c SIGTRAP, like \c SIGSEGV, are never masked and cannot have
    %Qore-language signal handlers.  See @ref signal_handling.

    @par Programs Started by the Process Inherit No File, Socket or Pipe
    Files, sockets and @ref Qore::File::getPipe() "File::getPipe()" pipes are close-on-exec, so a command that needs
    one of these descriptors must be given it explicitly, for example as its standard input or output.  See
    @ref file_close_on_exec.

    @par SIGHUP Ends a Process Without a Handler
    A program with no \c SIGHUP handler now ends on a hangup, with exit code 129; before, the signal had no effect.  The
    exception is a program started with \c SIGHUP ignored, for example under \c nohup, which still ignores it; the same
    now holds for \c SIGTERM.  A program that needs to survive a hangup should set a handler with
    @ref Qore::set_signal_handler() "set_signal_handler()".  See @ref signal_handling.

    @par The Signal Thread No Longer Takes SIGCHLD
    The signal handling thread does not wait for \c SIGCHLD, so components that watch their children through it (ex:
    the \c process module on macOS) receive it.  See @ref signal_thread_sigchld.

    @par An Empty String Is Found in Every String
    An empty search string is found at every position of a string: <tt>"Hello".contains("")</tt> is @ref True "True",
    and @ref Qore::index() "index()", \c find(), \c rfind() and the other search functions return a position instead
    of \c -1; a loop that searches for a possibly empty string must check for it.  See @ref Qore::index() "index()".

    @par A Hash Key Cannot Contain a NUL Character
    A hash key or member name with a NUL character raises \c HASH-KEY-ERROR instead of naming the key cut at the NUL
    character, and the data parsers raise their own parse error for such a key.  See @ref hash_key_nul.

    @par A Path Cannot Contain a NUL Character
    Every API that passes a string to the operating system as a path, a socket target, a module name or a time zone
    region refuses a string with a NUL character, with its error code and \c EINVAL.  See @ref path_nul_characters.

    @par Time Zones Do Not Open Files That a Program May Not Access
    A zoneinfo path is not opened under @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "PO_NO_FILESYSTEM", and a region with a \c ".."
    or empty path component or a backslash raises \c TZINFO-ERROR.  See @ref sandbox_fs_tz_regions.

    @par Local Times Follow All of the Data in Zoneinfo Files
    Time zones use all of the data in zoneinfo files, so local times before 1901 and after 2037 and with "slim" files
    change, and an invalid file raises \c TZINFO-ERROR.  See @ref zoneinfo_files.

    @par getpwuid2(), getpwnam2() and getgrnam2() Raise Their Documented Error Codes
    @ref Qore::getpwuid2() "getpwuid2()", @ref Qore::getpwnam2() "getpwnam2()" and
    @ref Qore::getgrnam2() "getgrnam2()" raise \c GETPWUID2-ERROR, \c GETPWNAM2-ERROR and \c GETGRNAM2-ERROR instead
    of the misspelled \c GETPPWUID2-ERROR, \c GETPPWNAM2-ERROR and \c GETPGRNAM2-ERROR.

    @par A String With a NUL Character Is Refused Where It Would Be Cut
    A string with a NUL character that would be cut where it is passed to the operating system, a C library or a
    network protocol raises an error (or fails with \c EINVAL) instead of being truncated, and received HTTP/1.x
    header lines with a NUL character are refused; see @ref string_nul_characters.

    @par A Name With a NUL Character Names No Function, Method, Class or Other Identifier
    A function, method, class, variable, type or other identifier name given as a runtime string (for example to
    @ref Qore::call_function() "call_function()", @ref Qore::call_object_method() "call_object_method()", the
    @ref Qore::Program "Program" and reflection APIs, or deserialization) is not found if it contains a NUL
    character, instead of being looked up with the name cut there; see @ref string_nul_characters.

    @par A CR, LF or Other Control Character Is Refused in HTTP Message Lines and FTP Commands
    HTTP methods, request targets, versions, status messages and header names and values cannot contain a control
    character other than horizontal tab, and FTP commands, remote paths, user names and passwords cannot contain CR
    or LF; such a string raises the API's error before anything is sent; see @ref string_line_characters.

    @par Mail Is Sent With SMTP Transparency, and a Line Break Cannot Start Another Mail Command or Header
    Messages are always sent with SMTP transparency, header values with line breaks are encoded, and addresses,
    header names, SASL credentials and POP3 commands with characters that would end their line or field raise an
    error; @ref MailMessage::Message::getFrom() "Message::getFrom()" of a parsed message returns the sender's
    address and @ref MailMessage::Message::getSender() "Message::getSender()" the \c From header.  See
    @ref mailmessage_v1_5_1, @ref smtpclient_v2_2, @ref mime_1_9 and @ref pop3client_v2_1.

    @par Modules Export Only Their Own Declarations and Reexported Modules
    Constants and namespaces that a module received from modules it imported with a plain @ref requires "%requires"
    or loaded at runtime are no longer exported to the importing @ref Qore::Program "Program"; a program using such
    a constant must require the module declaring it, unless a module it requires reexports it; see
    @ref user_module_exports.

    @par HttpServer Labels Responses by Their Data and With Registered Charset Names
    @ref httpserverintro "HttpServer" sends a binary response body without a \c Content-Type as
    \c application/octet-stream without a charset, and labels text with the registered charset name in lower case
    (such as \c utf-8 instead of \c utf8; see @ref HttpServer::http_get_charset_label() "http_get_charset_label()");
    see @ref http1_7.

    @par HTTP Message Bodies Are Decoded by the Media Type
    @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient", @ref restclientintro "RestClient" and @ref httpserverintro "HttpServer"
    decide whether a body is text, and its character encoding, by the rules of its media type (JSON is always UTF-8,
    XML uses its declaration, binary types stay binary, and a body without a \c Content-Type is sniffed); see
    @ref Qore::get_http_body_encoding() "get_http_body_encoding()".  @ref Qore::HTTPClient::get() "HTTPClient::get()"
    and @ref Qore::HTTPClient::post() "HTTPClient::post()" return \c *data, so code that assigns a response that can
    be binary to a \c string variable must use \c data or \c auto instead.

    @par Data Provider Indexes Are Published Without the Canonical Index File
    Indexes are published without \c qore-data-index.yaml unless it is requested; earlier versions of
    @ref providerindexutilintro "ProviderIndexUtil" cannot read such an index, and a canonical index file larger
    than 128 MiB is not read by default; rebuild indexes after upgrading; see @ref providerindexutil_v1_2.

    @par ProviderIndexUtil Returns Indexed Actions Without Type Objects
    \c tryGetApproximateActionsForApp() and \c tryGetApproximateAppAction() are removed; use
    @ref ProviderIndexUtil::ProviderIndexUtil::tryGetActionMetadataForApp() "tryGetActionMetadataForApp()" and
    @ref ProviderIndexUtil::ProviderIndexUtil::tryGetActionMetadata() "tryGetActionMetadata()", which describe option
    types with plain data; see @ref providerindexutil_v1_2.

    @par PostgreSQL Lists Only the Connection's Schemas
    On PostgreSQL, tables, views, sequences and other objects are listed only from the schemas of the connection's
    \c search_path, and unqualified only when they are in <tt>current_schema()</tt>; use a connection whose
    \c search_path names any other schemas needed; see @ref pgsql_schema_scope.

    @par Schema Alignment Drops Indexes That the Template Does Not Have
    Aligning a table or schema with @ref sqlutilintro "SqlUtil" drops indexes that the template does not have again,
    as before SqlUtil 1.7.2; add such an index to the template or use the \c omit option to keep it; see
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getAlignSql() "AbstractTable::getAlignSql()" and @ref sqlutilv3_11_0.

    @par Stream Socket Binding and Address Reporting
    A stream socket bound on all interfaces is one dual-stack socket on \c "::" (a port in use in either address
    family raises \c SOCKET-BIND-ERROR), a socket bound to a host name holds its port in all the name's address
    families, and IPv4 peers are reported with their IPv4 addresses and @ref Qore::AF_INET "AF_INET"; see
    @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()".

    @par The Initial Thread Keeps the Process Name on Linux
    On Linux, the initial thread is no longer renamed <tt>qore/1</tt>, so \c ps, \c pidof and \c killall show the
    process name, which @ref Qore::get_thread_name() "get_thread_name()" returns in that thread.

    @par Parse Depth Limit
    Code nested more than 1000 syntax elements deep, such as an expression adding more than about 1000 terms with
    \c +, raises a \c PARSE-EXCEPTION; see @ref parse_nesting_limit.

    @par Complex Type Nesting Limit
    Complex container and reference types nested more than 64 levels deep have \c auto as their value type; see
    @ref list_complex_type.

    @par Parse-Time Type Narrowing
    The element and value types of variables declared as @ref list_type "list<auto>" or
    @ref hash_type "hash<auto>" are narrowed at parse time, so a later assignment of an incompatible type raises a
    \c PARSE-TYPE-ERROR; use @ref auto_no_narrow "auto!" or the
    @ref broken-narrowed-types "%broken-narrowed-types" parse directive to keep the old behavior.

    @par New typedef and enum Keywords
    The @ref typedef_keyword "typedef" and @ref enums "enum" keywords conflict with identifiers of the same names;
    use the @ref no-typedef "%no-typedef" and @ref no-enum "%no-enum" parse directives for such code.

    @par Namespace Resolution Changes
    Unqualified symbol resolution prefers symbols in the @ref Qore "Qore" namespace over user-defined namespaces; use
    @ref broken-namespace-resolution "%broken-namespace-resolution" to restore the old resolution; see
    @ref unqualified_symbol_resolution.

    @par Removal of \%strict-types Support
    The @ref strict-types "%strict-types" and @ref loose-types "%loose-types" parse directives have no effect, and
    @ref Qore::PO_STRICT_TYPES "PO_STRICT_TYPES" is \c 0, so typed variables are not initialized with default
    values.

    @par Build System
    @parblock
    - a C++20 compiler is required
    - the autotools build system is removed; CMake is the only build system (see the \c BUILDING file)
    - module documentation generates Java bindings, which require JNI; configure with
      \c -DQORE_GENERATE_JAVA_BINDINGS=OFF to build without them (see the \c BUILDING file)
    - adding or removing a source file of a separated user module reconfigures the build, so the file is installed
    - \c Qore::CFLAGS keeps reusable compiler options with their quoting, without build-machine prefix maps
    @endparblock

    @par Mime::MimeTypeYaml Is the RFC 9512 Type application/yaml
    @ref Mime::MimeTypeYaml "Mime::MimeTypeYaml" is \c "application/yaml" instead of \c "text/x-yaml"; code that
    sends YAML with it to a %Qore server before 3.0 must send \c "text/x-yaml", and code comparing received media
    types should use @ref Mime::YamlMimeTypes "Mime::YamlMimeTypes"; see @ref mime_1_9.

    @par JSON Numbers Must Have the Form That RFC 8259 Defines
    @ref Qore::Json::parse_json() "parse_json()" raises \c JSON-PARSE-ERROR for a number that is not valid JSON
    (ex: \c "01", \c ".5", \c "1.") and for text after a \c null value, and returns an integer outside the range of
    an \c int as a \c number; code that expects an \c int for every JSON integer must accept a \c number; see
    @ref jsonserializationguide.

    @par The linenoise Completion Callback Completes the Word at the Cursor
    The callback set with @ref Qore::Linenoise::set_callback() "Linenoise::set_callback()" receives the line up to
    the cursor, and each string that it returns replaces the word before the cursor instead of the whole line, so a
    callback must return completed words; see @ref line_completion and the
    @ref linenoisereleasehistory "linenoise release notes" for the changes since the separate module's version 1.0.1.

    @par LoggerAppenderQueue Default Memory Limit
    A \c LoggerAppenderQueue created without arguments has a 10 MiB memory limit and blocks producers when it is
    full; pass \c -1 to the constructor for an unlimited queue; see @ref logger_backpressure.

    @par Soft Type NULL Value Handling
    The non-optional soft types (such as @ref softint_type "softint" or @ref softstring_type "softstring") refuse
    SQL \c NULL with \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR instead of converting it to their zero or empty value, and
    @ref softlist_type "softlist" converts it to an empty list; use an optional soft type (ex: \c *softint), or the
    @ref broken-soft-types "%broken-soft-types" parse option to restore the earlier behavior.

    @par \%modern Is Now the Default for Non-.q Files
    Source files with any extension other than <tt>.q</tt>, or with none, are parsed in @ref modern "%modern" mode
    with all non-strict warnings, so legacy code in such files must be renamed to <tt>.q</tt>, get explicit parse
    directives such as @ref old-style "%old-style", or be run with the \c QORE_OLD_STYLE_DEFAULT environment variable
    set; see @ref modern "%modern".

    @par Compatibility Parse Options Refused Under \%modern
    Parse directives and options that change the meaning of code, such as @ref no-negative-offsets "%no-negative-offsets" and
    the \c %%broken-* options, cannot be used while @ref modern "%modern" is in effect; legacy code that needs them is
    parsed without @ref modern "%modern" (see @ref modern_option_consistency).

    @par summarize Statement Rejected Under \%modern
    @ref modern "%modern" implies @ref no-summarize "%no-summarize", so the \c summarize statement fails to parse in
    files parsed in @ref modern "%modern" mode; rewrite it as a @ref context "context" loop or use
    @ref new-style "%new-style" instead of @ref modern "%modern".

    @anchor qore_3_0_0_regex_errors
    @par Regular Expression Resource Limits Now Raise an Exception
    A match that PCRE2 abandons because the pattern needs more backtracking than its limits allow (ex: <tt>(a+)+</tt>)
    raises \c REGEX-ERROR from every regular expression operator, function and method instead of reporting no match;
    see @ref qore_regex_limits.

    @par Changed "%y" Output for Dates, Non-Finite Numbers and Objects
    The \c "%y" and \c "%Y" @ref string_formatting "format specifiers" render absolute dates as YAML timestamps
    without the weekday and zone name, non-finite floats and numbers as \c ".nan" and \c ".inf", and the members of
    an object as a nested mapping (<tt>{&lt;C object&gt;: {a: 1}}</tt>); use \c "%n" where the earlier output is
    required; see @ref yaml_format_specifications.

    @par ProgramControl and Breakpoint Objects Are Created for Each Call
    The methods that return @ref Qore::ProgramControl "ProgramControl" and @ref Qore::Breakpoint "Breakpoint" objects
    return a new object for each call, so such objects must be compared by their IDs, not by identity; see
    @ref programcontrol_object_identity.

    @par C++ API Changes for Binary Modules
    @parblock
    The following changes of the C++ API can require changes to binary modules; each is described in the C++ API
    documentation of the named classes and functions:
    - the printf-style functions (such as <tt>ExceptionSink::raiseException()</tt> and <tt>QoreString::sprintf()</tt>)
      are declared with \c QORE_PRINTF_FORMAT(), so arguments that do not match the format get a \c -Wformat warning
    - \c QoreProgram::getProgramId() and \c QoreProgram::resolveProgramId() use the 64-bit
      \c qore_program_id_t, and \c QoreProgram::registerQoreObject() and related functions do nothing
    - \c QoreHttpClientObject and \c QoreHTTPClient have protected destructors: allocate them on the heap and release
      them with \c deref()
    - the non-blocking connection of \c QoreSocket::startConnect() resolves the host name asynchronously: wait also
      for \c AbstractPollState::getExtraWaitFds() and at most for \c AbstractPollState::getPollTimeoutMs()
    - \c AbstractQoreProgramExternalData::copy() is called without the parent Program's lock and can run in several
      threads at once (see "Data of a Module in a Program" in the module development guide)
    - a value returned by builtin code together with an exception is released, so API callers receive no value
      with an exception (see \c q_func_t)
    - \c QoreFile::write() returns \c int64
    @endparblock

    @par qget Command-Line Changes
    The rewritten \c qget follows \c wget and \c curl for its options, uses a \c Content-Disposition file name only
    with \c --content-disposition, fails on HTTP error responses with distinct exit codes, and deprecates \c -P and
    \c -e; see the \c qget(1) manual page or \c qget \c --help.

    @par HTTPS Targets Through an HTTPS Proxy Use an End-to-End TLS Tunnel
    @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" reaches an HTTPS server through an HTTPS proxy with a \c CONNECT tunnel, so the
    proxy no longer sees the requests and the client certificate is sent only to the server; see
    @ref Qore::HTTPClient::setProxySecure() "HTTPClient::setProxySecure()".

    @par URI Query Strings Are Decoded When Parsed and Encoded When Made
    @ref Util::parse_uri_query() "parse_uri_query()" URL-decodes and @ref Util::make_uri_query() "make_uri_query()"
    URL-encodes the names and values of query parameters, so code must no longer decode or encode them itself; the
    @ref httpserverintro "HttpServer" module decodes only the resource path of a request, so a handler that splits
    the query string itself must decode its parameters.

    @par Character Encoding Changes
    @parblock
    - strings in stateful encodings (such as UTF-7 and ISO-2022-JP) are handled by their characters and are not
      compatible with ASCII; byte-position functions raise \c UNSUPPORTED-ENCODING for them (see @ref known_encodings)
    - the generic \c "UTF-32" encoding is big-endian with no byte order mark on every platform; use \c "UTF-32LE"
      for little-endian text (see @ref known_encodings)
    - Shift_JIS and its variants (such as \c "CP932") are not compatible with ASCII (see @ref known_encodings)
    - a default encoding that is not compatible with ASCII is refused at startup, or replaced by UTF-8 when it comes
      from the locale (see @ref default_encoding)
    @endparblock

    @par On macOS, Binds Are Refused Ports That Other Sockets Hold as on Linux
    On macOS, a bind on all interfaces is refused a port that another program uses for IPv4, and a bind of a specific
    address is refused a port that another socket holds on a wildcard address, with \c SOCKET-BIND-ERROR as on Linux;
    a server on a specific address must use another port than a server of the same user on all interfaces; see
    @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()".

    @par qpp Fails the Build for Every Error It Reports
    \c qpp exits with an error status and removes its output files when it has reported an error, such as a function
    with functional domains or code flags but no long comment, so such a binary module source must be fixed for the
    build to succeed; see the \c qpp(1) manual page.

    @par REST Schema and Data Provider Types
    @parblock
    - the options and fields of REST schema actions enforce the constraints of their schema (such as \c maxLength or
      \c enum) before the request is sent (see @ref restschemavalidator_data_types)
    - REST request types have no fields for \c readOnly properties, which are refused with \c TYPE-VALUE-ERROR
      (see @ref restschemavalidator_data_types)
    - the documented \c default of an API property is a remote default value, which is shown and never sent (see
      @ref dataprovider_remote_default_values)
    - nullable enumerations offer their choices, and optional fields with choices accept no value and \c NULL (see
      @ref restschemavalidator_data_types)
    - a record type whose fields all have default values describes itself as optional (see
      @ref hashdatatype_optional_by_defaults)
    @endparblock

    @par Minus-Equals With a Date Operand on an Integer Lvalue
    <tt>-=</tt> with a date operand on an integer lvalue assigns the date result of <tt>x - d</tt>, which an \c int
    lvalue refuses with \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR, instead of subtracting the date's integer value; see
    @ref minus_equals_operator.

    @par Indexing a String Gives a char
    <tt>s[i]</tt> on a string returns a @ref char "char" instead of a one-character string; it converts to a string
    wherever one is expected, but code that checks the type of the value sees \c char; see @ref char.

    @par cast<> to a Container of auto Is a Parse Error
    <tt>cast&lt;hash&lt;auto&gt;&gt;(...)</tt>, <tt>cast&lt;hash&lt;string, auto&gt;&gt;(...)</tt> and
    <tt>cast&lt;list&lt;auto&gt;&gt;(...)</tt> are refused at parse time, as they convert nothing; use
    <tt>cast&lt;hash&gt;</tt>, <tt>cast&lt;list&gt;</tt> or <tt>cast&lt;softlist&lt;auto&gt;&gt;</tt> instead; see
    @ref cast_auto_containers.

    @par Compound Assignments to Or-Nothing Lvalues Checked at Parse Time
    A compound assignment to an or-nothing lvalue (ex: <tt>*string s; s -= 1;</tt>) is refused with
    \c PARSE-TYPE-ERROR wherever it is refused for the plain type; see @ref compound_assignment_parse_checks.

    @par Reflection Follows the Privileges of the Calling Code
    The @ref reflectionintro "reflection" module raises \c REFLECTION-ACCESS-ERROR when code without full access to
    another @ref Qore::Program "Program" reads or writes its values, calls its code, or creates objects in it; see
    @ref reflection_caller_privilege (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5483">issue 5483</a>).

    @par CsvUtil Writes and Reads RFC 4180 CSV Data by Default
    The @ref csvutilintro "CsvUtil" writers escape quotes by doubling them and the readers read a backslash as an
    ordinary character; set the \c quote_escape option to a backslash to read or write files in the earlier format;
    see @ref csvutil_rfc4180.

    @par Destructors on a Cancelled or Interrupted Thread
    The destructor of a trusted class runs with cancellation and @ref sandbox_interrupt "program interrupts" deferred,
    so it completes, the destructor of a sandboxed class is skipped while a cancellation, an interrupt or a sandbox
    limit is pending, and sandboxed code never runs with cancellation deferred; see @ref cancel_destructors and
    @ref cancel_security (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5634">issue 5634</a>).

    @subsection qore_3_0_0_new_features New Features in Qore
    @par Program Thread Coordination
    @ref Qore::Program::waitForThreads() "Program::waitForThreads()" and
    @ref Qore::Program::waitForThread() "waitForThread()" provide cancellable, condition-driven waits for Program
    activity. @ref Qore::Program::cancelThread() "cancelThread()" and
    @ref Qore::Program::cancelThreads() "cancelThreads()" request cooperative cancellation scoped to that Program.
    The all-thread request includes starting threads and descendants until the Program becomes idle. These APIs
    allow an embedding UI to keep responding while waiting for the Program to become safe to parse or reset;
    they do not enable concurrent parsing or forcibly kill native threads.

    @par Program Thread Lifecycle Events
    @ref Qore::Program::subscribeThreadEvents() "Program::subscribeThreadEvents()" delivers events to a
    @ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue" when the Program's background threads start, are asked to cancel and end (with
    how they ended), and when the Program becomes idle or active
    (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5633">issue 5633</a>).

    @par Waiting for a Thread to Terminate
    @ref Qore::thread_join() "thread_join()" waits until a thread started with @ref background has left the thread
    list, which is later than a Counter signalled from @ref on_exit; see the teardown order in its documentation
    (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5575">issue 5575</a>).

    @par Exit Hooks
    register_exit_hook() registers code that the library calls before it waits for background threads to end, at the
    end of the process or when a @ref Qore::Program "Program" is deleted, so that a module or a program can stop a
    thread that runs until it is told to stop instead of keeping the process from ending (see
    @ref user_module_exit_hooks)

    @par System Thread Capabilities
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::createSystemThreadCall() "SandboxManager::createSystemThreadCall()" lets a module start a
    fixed operation in a thread that no sandbox governs, and @ref Qore::SandboxManager::isSandboxed()
    "SandboxManager::isSandboxed()" tells trusted code whether the code it serves is sandboxed

    @par Module Initialization Belongs to No Sandbox
    A module from the process-wide module path is initialized as library code, with no sandbox, also when sandboxed
    code loads it first; every later call into it is governed by its caller (see @ref sandbox_module_init)

    @par Sandboxed Module Loads Cannot Replace Library Modules
    Sandboxed and restricted code can no longer extend the process-wide module path, replace a library module, or
    choose the modules and parse defines that a library module uses; a module that it loads from elsewhere is used
    only by its own sandbox (see @ref sandbox_module_init)

    @par Reflection Member Access
    Code that a sandbox governs, and code whose parse options restrict a functional domain, may use the
    \c private and \c private:internal elements of a class through the @ref reflectionintro "reflection" module
    only where the language allows it (see @ref reflection_member_access)

    @par HTTP Server Fast Routes
    A path can be registered with @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addFastRoute() "addFastRoute()" so that the async I/O
    thread answers its qualifying requests in microseconds, with no worker hand-off and a response identical to the
    handler's; a @ref Qore::FastCell "FastCell" or a @ref Qore::FastResponseSlot "FastResponseSlot" that Qore code sets
    selects or supplies the response without waiting.  See @ref Qore::HttpFastRoutes "HttpFastRoutes".

    @par Thread Entry and Exit Handlers
    @ref Qore::Program::setThreadEntryHandler() "Program::setThreadEntryHandler()" sets code that runs every time a
    thread enters and leaves a Program, so that an embedding application can keep a context of its own for each
    Program on threads that run the code of many Programs, such as async I/O callback workers.

    @par Module Parse Option Masks
    A process can let code with fewer privileges load trusted modules that use more functionality with a module
    parse option mask, which strips restrictions from what modules inherit from their loader or adds restrictions to
    them (ex: set_module_parse_option_mask()); see @ref module_parse_option_masks.

    @par LLVM-backed Execution Modes and AOT Compilation
    One IR pipeline serves the @ref exec_mode_ir "IR interpreter", the LLVM @ref exec_mode_jit "JIT",
    @ref exec_mode_tiered "tiered" execution (the default for @ref modern "%modern" code) and
    @ref exec_mode_aot "AOT" compilation with the new @ref qcc_tool "qcc" compiler, which builds executables, binary
    user modules (<tt>.qmod</tt>), objects and archives; standard user modules are installed
    @ref aot_modules "AOT-compiled", and non-modern code keeps @ref exec_mode_ast "AST" execution.  LLVM 16 or later
    is required; see @ref execution_modes "Execution Modes".

    @par New Required Libraries and Async Networking Stack
    Building %Qore requires Brotli, Zstd, LZ4, Eigen3, ICU, libprotobuf, Kerberos 5 (GSSAPI) and LibYAML, and the
    async networking stack (nghttp2, ngtcp2, nghttp3 and c-ares for asynchronous DNS), which the build downloads
    and links statically when no adequate system installation is found; ONNX Runtime is optional.  The
    @ref Qore::Option "Option" constants \c HAVE_BROTLI, \c HAVE_ZSTD, \c HAVE_LZ4, \c HAVE_HTTP2 and
    \c HAVE_HTTP3 are always \c True; see the \c BUILDING file for versions, package names, pinned source
    archives, mirrors and reproducible builds.

    @par Standard Descriptors at Startup
    \c qore, \c qcc and library initialization open a missing standard file descriptor on the null device before
    opening any other file, so a script or data file can never receive output meant for standard output or error;
    see @ref cmdline_std_descriptors "Command-Line Processing".

    @anchor qore_3_0_0_http_socket_behavior
    @par HTTP and Socket Behavior
    @parblock
    - new @ref Qore::get_http_body_encoding() "get_http_body_encoding()" and
      @ref Qore::decode_http_body() "decode_http_body()" apply the rules that all HTTP clients and servers use to
      find the encoding of a text body, including RFC 9110 parsing of the \c charset parameter
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5469">issue 5469</a>)
    - new @ref Qore::Socket::getQuicSSLCipherName() "Socket::getQuicSSLCipherName()" and
      @ref Qore::Socket::getQuicSSLCipherVersion() "Socket::getQuicSSLCipherVersion()" for HTTP/3 QUIC sessions
    - new @ref Qore::Socket::shutdownWrite() "Socket::shutdownWrite()" shuts down the sending side of a connection
      only (a half-close): the peer reads the end of the stream, and the response that the peer sends can still be
      read; a TLS connection sends its \c close_notify alert first
    - a \c QOREADDRINFO-GETINFO-ERROR exception now gives the resolver's status in <tt>arg.status</tt>, so that a
      name that does not exist (\c "ENOTFOUND") can be told from a temporary failure such as \c "ETIMEOUT"; also
      raised by @ref Qore::getaddrinfo_async() "getaddrinfo_async()" (see @ref Qore::getaddrinfo() "getaddrinfo()")
    @endparblock

    @par astparser Uses a tree-sitter Parser
    The @ref astparserintro "astparser" module parses %Qore source with a tree-sitter grammar; the generated parser
    is in the source distribution, so the Rust toolchain and the tree-sitter CLI are needed only to change the
    grammar (see the \c BUILDING file).

    @par Program-owned Module Path Directives
    @ref prepend-module-path "%prepend-module-path" and the new quoted form of
    @ref append-module-path "%append-module-path" let a program declare its own module search path, which is kept in
    @ref exec_mode_aot "AOT" artifacts; see also @ref no-module-path-directives "%no-module-path-directives".

    @par Child Modules for Extensible Base Modules
    The new @ref try-child-module "%try-child-module" directive lets a user module declare optional child modules
    that extend it and are loaded after it; a missing child is ignored and a broken one is reported without failing
    the parent; see @ref user_module_children "Child Modules".

    @par Class Members and Static Variables Backed by Module Storage
    A binary module can declare a static class variable or a normal member whose every read and write calls the
    module's accessor functions (\c QoreClass::addBuiltinStaticVarWithAccessors() and
    \c QoreClass::addBuiltinMemberWithAccessors() in the C++ API), so it mirrors a value the module owns, such as a
    Java field in the jni module.

    @par Versioned C++ APIs Between Binary Modules
    A binary module can publish a versioned C++ API that other binary modules resolve at run time with
    \c q_get_module_cpp_api(), which raises a %Qore exception on a version mismatch, so an optional C++ dependency
    between modules is possible; see "Sharing a C++ API Between Modules" in the C++ API documentation.

    @par Cross-Module Links in External Module Documentation
    The %Qore SDK installs reference indexes of the language and bundled module documentation, so the documentation
    of an external module can link to %Qore types and to the modules named in \c QORE_DOXYGEN_MODULES; see
    "Module Documentation and Cross-Module Links" in the C++ API documentation.

    @par Opt-In Structured Datasource Mutation Observer
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource" and @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool "DatasourcePool" objects accept a
    mutation observer, set with
    @ref Qore::SQL::AbstractDatasource::setMutationObserver() "AbstractDatasource::setMutationObserver()", that
    receives the start and outcome of every operation and can reject a mutation before it runs, with metadata
    declared by the caller with @ref Qore::SQL::SqlMutationDeclaration "SqlMutationDeclaration"; see
    @ref sql_mutation_admission "Observer Semantics".

    @par Language and Runtime
    @parblock
    - @ref modern "%modern" bundles new-style syntax, required types, strict arguments, strong encapsulation and all
      warnings; it is the recommended way to write new code, and files with the <tt>.qr</tt> extension enable it
    - strict @ref ambiguous-call-resolution and @ref ambiguous-overload warnings, enabled with
      @ref strict-warnings "%strict-warnings", \c --strict-warnings or \c WARN_STRICT
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/55">issue 55</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/188">issue 188</a>)
    - zero-cost \c \@assert and \c \@debug statements, compiled only with @ref enable-debug "%enable-debug"
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5018">issue 5018</a>)
    - new @ref enums "enum" and @ref typedef_keyword "typedef" keywords for type-safe enumerations and type aliases
    - new type syntax: @ref code_complex_type "typed callables", @ref union_type "union types",
      @ref auto_no_narrow "auto!" without type narrowing, and @ref date_type "absolute and relative date types"
    - new @ref char "char" type for single Unicode codepoints with \c c'...' literals; a single string index and
      string iteration yield \c char values (@ref no-string-index-char "%no-string-index-char" restores strings)
    - new @ref string_escape_chars "Unicode string escapes" \c \\u{...}, \c \\uXXXX and \c \\UXXXXXXXX
    - @ref negative-offsets "%negative-offsets" for from-end indexes and slices, implied by @ref modern "%modern"
    - @ref function_named_arguments "named arguments" in calls of functions, methods and constructors
    - \c const for @ref constants "typed constants", @ref readonly_local_variables "read-only local variables" and
      parameters, and @ref const_methods "const methods"
    - <tt>?:</tt> as an alias of the @ref value_coalescing_operator "value coalescing operator"
    - the @ref opaque_assignment_operator "opaque reference assignment operator (\@=)" for references that the
      garbage collector does not scan
    - <tt>cast&lt;hash&lt;auto&gt;&gt;</tt> and <tt>cast&lt;list&lt;auto&gt;&gt;</tt> are parse errors as no-op
      casts (@ref broken-auto-cast "%broken-auto-cast" accepts them)
    - the \c %%Y pseudo-YAML @ref string_formatting "format specifier", and
      @ref Qore::format_bounded() "format_bounded()" and related functions for formatting arbitrarily large values
    - values are NaN-boxed in 8 bytes with short strings stored inline, transparently to code
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5084">issue 5084</a>)
    - HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 with fully asynchronous I/O, used by @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" without code changes
      and controlled with @ref Qore::HTTPClient::setHttp2Mode() "setHttp2Mode()" and
      @ref Qore::HTTPClient::setHttp3Mode() "setHttp3Mode()"; see also @ref httpclientiointro "HttpClientIo"
    - @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" for filesystem, network, thread and
      per-call @ref sandbox_resource_limits "wall time", @ref sandbox_cpu_limit "CPU time" and
      @ref sandbox_stack_limit "stack size" limits, a @ref sandbox_memory_limit "memory limit" and safe interruption of
      running code; see
      @ref sandboxing (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5000">issue 5000</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5581">issue 5581</a>)
    - @ref thread_cancellation "cooperative thread cancellation" with @ref Qore::cancel_thread() "cancel_thread()"
    - threading primitives @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future", @ref Qore::Thread::Channel "Channel",
      @ref Qore::Thread::WaitGroup "WaitGroup", @ref Qore::Thread::Semaphore "Semaphore",
      @ref Qore::call_async() "call_async()" and @ref Qore::parallel_map() "parallel_map()"; see
      @ref thread_synchronization
    - debugger APIs for statements, step-in targets, local variables and expression evaluation in
      @ref Qore::ProgramControl "ProgramControl"; see @ref debugging_api
    - @ref Qore::ParseOptions "ParseOptions" for parse options beyond 64 bits
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5145">issue 5145</a>)
    - an @ref repl_mode "interactive REPL" in the \c qore binary, with @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_REPARSE "PO_ALLOW_REPARSE"
      for incremental parsing of one @ref Qore::Program "Program"
    - the @ref garbage_collection "garbage collector" collects cycles through @ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue",
      @ref Qore::TreeMap "TreeMap" and binary-module data, skips unchanged graphs, and reports its scans with
      @ref Qore::get_scan_stats() "get_scan_stats()"
    - pre-compiled regular expression classes @ref Qore::Regex "Regex", @ref Qore::RegexSubst "RegexSubst" and
      @ref Qore::RegexExtract "RegexExtract"
    - @ref Qore::Dir "Dir" methods such as @ref Qore::Dir::listNames() "listNames()" with concrete return types
    - size limits for untrusted data: the \c max_size argument of the decompression functions,
      @ref Qore::HTTPClient::setMaxResponseBodySize() "HTTPClient::setMaxResponseBodySize()", and the
      \c max_event_size argument of @ref Qore::HTTPClient::readServerSentEvent() "readServerSentEvent()"
    - non-blocking I/O APIs: @ref Qore::AsyncIoController::exec() "AsyncIoController::exec()",
      @ref Qore::getaddrinfo_async() "getaddrinfo_async()", HTTP response methods of @ref Qore::Socket "Socket" such
      as @ref Qore::Socket::startPollLingeringClose() "startPollLingeringClose()", and
      @ref Qore::PollPipeline::addRecvImapResponse() "PollPipeline::addRecvImapResponse()"
    - other new functions and methods: @ref Qore::deflate_raw() "deflate_raw()" and the raw inflate functions
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5065">issue 5065</a>),
      @ref Qore::Thread::Queue::tryGet() "Queue::tryGet()", @ref Qore::check_line_text() "check_line_text()",
      @ref Qore::SQL::make_datasource_string() "make_datasource_string()",
      @ref Qore::get_value_byte_size() "get_value_byte_size()", @ref <binary>::toHexArray() "<binary>::toHexArray()",
      and @ref Qore::ReadOnlyFile::readShortBinary() "ReadOnlyFile::readShortBinary()"
    @endparblock

    @par Generic Builtin Classes and Result Records
    The thread value containers (@ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue<T>", @ref Qore::Thread::Channel "Channel<T>",
    @ref Qore::Thread::Promise "Promise<T>", @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future<T>"), @ref Qore::TreeMap "TreeMap<V>",
    the iterators, the poll operations and result hashdecls such as @ref Qore::KeyValueInfo<V> "KeyValueInfo<T>" are
    generic, runtime-checked types; raw declarations such as <tt>Queue q()</tt> remain valid and mean
    <tt>Queue&lt;auto&gt;</tt>.  See @ref generic_classes "Generic Classes".

    @par Source-Defined Generic Classes
    Classes, hashdecls, functions and methods declared in %Qore can have type parameters, with defaults, upper bounds,
    wildcard type arguments, type-argument inference and attributes for interoperating with unparameterized code;
    generic types are preserved by serialization, reflection and the API metadata tools.  See
    @ref generic_classes "Generic Classes" and @ref hashdecl_generic "Generic Hashdecls".

    @par Fast Text Processing APIs
    @ref <string>::getByte() "<string>::getByte()", the @ref Qore::Scanner "Scanner" class and lazy string iterators
    such as @ref Qore::StringSplitIterator "StringSplitIterator" scan large strings without allocating intermediate
    values, and @ref map "map", @ref select "select" and @ref foldl "foldl" iterate the builtin iterators without a
    method call per element; see @ref fast_text_processing.

    @par Comprehensive Regular Expression Improvements
    Regular expressions support named capture groups, detailed match information
    (@ref Qore::RegexMatchInfo "RegexMatchInfo"), substitution with a callback, match counting and escaping
    (@ref Qore::regex_count() "regex_count()", @ref Qore::regex_escape() "regex_escape()"), the \c U option and the
    @ref Qore::RE_Ungreedy "RE_Ungreedy", @ref Qore::RE_DollarEndOnly "RE_DollarEndOnly",
    @ref Qore::RE_Anchored "RE_Anchored" and @ref Qore::RE_DupNames "RE_DupNames" options, Perl-style case conversion
    escapes (\c \\U, \c \\L, \c \\u, \c \\l, \c \\E) and \c $0 for the whole match in replacement strings.
    Substitution keeps embedded NUL characters, and @ref Qore::split() "split()" can keep a trailing empty field
    (see @ref split_trailing_empty_fields).  See @ref qore_regex.

    @par Unicode Case Conversion Fixes
    Case conversion (@ref <string>::lwr() "<string>::lwr()", @ref <string>::upr() "<string>::upr()",
    @ref Qore::tolower() "tolower()", @ref Qore::toupper() "toupper()", and \c QoreString::tolwr() and
    \c QoreString::toupr() in the C++ API) applies the complete Unicode full case mappings, including 1-to-many
    mappings and the \c Final_Sigma condition, and returns a character whose mapping the string's encoding cannot
    represent unchanged.  Conversions to an encoding that cannot represent a character raise
    \c ENCODING-CONVERSION-ERROR with every \c iconv library; see @ref <string>::lwr() "<string>::lwr()" and
    @ref character_encoding_overview.

    @par Brace-Delimited Regular Expressions
    All regular expression operators accept brace delimiters, as in <tt>m{pattern}</tt> and
    <tt>s{pattern}{replacement}</tt>, so patterns with forward slashes need no escapes; see
    @ref qore_regex_brace_delimiters.

    @par Dense Buffers and Typed DBI Results
    The new @ref buffer "buffer<T>" type stores numeric, boolean, string and decimal values without boxing, with
    views, elementwise operators and reductions;
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectTyped() "selectTyped()",
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRowsTyped() "selectRowsTyped()" and @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectColumnar() "selectColumnar()" return typed query
    results and @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult "ColumnarResult" columns that can be exchanged with Arrow and the
    @ref dataframeintro "dataframe" module.  See @ref buffer "Buffer".

    @par DBI Drivers Can Report an Error Closing a Connection
    A driver that registers the new \c QDBI_METHOD_CLOSE_SINK method can report a connection that it could not close
    cleanly: @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::close() "Datasource::close()" raises the error, or reports it as a warning
    event when the close cleans up after another error.  See @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::close() "Datasource::close()"
    and the DBI driver page of the C++ API documentation.

    @par C++ API: Module Threads and Callbacks Run Under the Caller's Sandbox
    A binary module can run work for sandboxed callers on its own worker or callback threads under each caller's
    @ref sandboxing "sandbox" with the new \c QoreSandboxCapture and \c QoreSandboxCaptureHelper classes, and check
    an operation against a captured policy on any thread; see
    @ref interruptible_io_sandbox_threads "Carrying the Caller's Sandbox onto Another Thread".

    @par Sandboxing I/O That Qore Does Not See
    Java code called through the \c jni module checks its own I/O with \c org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox; data provider
    apps declare I/O that the sandbox does not see with the new \c external_io and \c sandbox_aware fields, and
    sandboxed callers are refused the actions, factories and connections of an app that is not sandbox-aware (see
    @ref sandbox_external_io).

    @par C++ API: Cancellable Waits in Binary Modules
    \c qore_cancellable_poll() and the \c QoreCancelWakeupHelper class let a binary module's waits end when the
    thread is @ref thread_cancellation "cancelled" or its %Program is @ref sandbox_interrupt "interrupted"; see
    @ref interruptible_io_module_guide "Interruptible I/O in Binary Modules".

    @par C++ API: Declarations Report Their Program
    \c TypedHashDecl::getProgram() and \c QoreEnumDecl::getProgram() return the %Program that owns a declaration, and
    \c qore_register_program_cleanup_callback() lets a module release data it caches for a %Program's declarations
    (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5517">issue 5517</a>).

    @par Builtin Classes Can Hold Undeclared Members in External Storage
    A builtin class can handle writes to its undeclared members with a callback registered with
    \c QoreClass::setMemberWriteGate(), and its \c memberGate() method and write gate are inherited by %Qore
    subclasses; see @ref member_write_gate
    (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5511">issue 5511</a>).

    @par Experimental Plugin-Type ABI
    Binary modules can register plugin types with \c qore_register_plugin_types_v1(), with serialization,
    reflection through @ref Qore::Reflection::PluginRegistry "PluginRegistry", IR and LLVM lowering hooks, and the new
    @ref fp-fast-math "%fp-fast-math" parse directive; see @ref plugin_types.

    @par Module-Loading Lock Hierarchy
    Modules load concurrently, one thread initializing each module, and a dependency cycle between loading threads
    raises \c CIRCULAR-MODULE-DEPENDENCY instead of deadlocking; a module must not hold its private locks across the
    start of a module load.  See @ref module_load_concurrency.

    @par RFC 7231 / RFC 2822 Date Parsing
    @ref Qore::date(string) "date(string)" and @ref Qore::TimeZone::date(string) "TimeZone::date(string)" parse
    HTTP and email dates, and @ref Qore::TimeZone::date(string) "TimeZone::date(string)" raises \c INVALID-DATE for
    an invalid string; @ref <date>::getUtcOffset() "<date>::getUtcOffset()" returns the offset at the represented
    instant.  See @ref Qore::date(string) "date(string)".

    @par Extended Calendar Years
    Date strings accept negative years and years with more than four digits, and date calculations, formatting and
    @ref Qore::get_years() "get_years()" support the complete year; see @ref date_extended_years.

    @par Qualified Container Types in Serialized Data
    @ref Qore::Serializable "Serializable" writes namespace-qualified container types, so data deserializes to the
    class it was written from when two modules use the same class name, and releases rejected input safely; see
    @ref serializable_container_types and @ref serializable_rejected_input.

    @par RFC 3986 Reference Resolution and HTTP Redirect Metadata
    The new @ref Qore::resolve_url() "resolve_url()" function resolves URI references (RFC 3986 section 5.2),
    @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" reports the effective URL and the redirects followed (see
    @ref httpclient_redirects), and @ref filelocationhandlerintro "FileLocationHandler" can return a resource with
    this metadata (see @ref filelocationhandler_resources).

    @par Fixed HTTP Endpoint URLs
    The new @ref Util::get_http_endpoint_url() "get_http_endpoint_url()" function builds the URL of a fixed HTTP
    endpoint with a fallback path; see @ref http_client_migration_urls.

    @par Incremental Message Digests
    The new @ref Qore::Digest "Digest" class calculates a message digest from data added in parts.

    @par Private Certificate Authorities for TLS Clients
    @ref Qore::Socket::setSslCaLocations() "Socket::setSslCaLocations()" and the \c ssl_ca_file and \c ssl_ca_path
    options of @ref Qore::HTTPClient::constructor() "HTTPClient::constructor()" set the CA certificates that server
    certificates are verified against.

    @par Complete Certificate Serial Numbers
    The new @ref Qore::SSLCertificate::getSerialNumberHex() "SSLCertificate::getSerialNumberHex()" returns serial
    numbers of any size in hexadecimal.

    @par FTP File Sizes and Restarted Transfers
    @ref Qore::FtpClient::size() "FtpClient::size()" returns the size of a remote file, and
    @ref Qore::FtpClient::get() "FtpClient::get()" with an offset resumes an interrupted download.

    @par FTP Transfers to Any Output Stream
    @ref Qore::FtpClient::get() "FtpClient::get()" writes to an output stream of any class, including one implemented
    in %Qore, and a failed transfer closes the control connection; see @ref Qore::FtpClient "FtpClient".

    @par qget Rewritten on Async I/O
    \c qget is based on the new @ref urldownloaderintro "UrlDownloader" module: it downloads over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2,
    HTTP/3, FTP, FTPS and any @ref filelocationhandlerintro "FileLocationHandler" scheme with a progress display,
    <tt>.part</tt> files and TLS and proxy options; see \c qget \c --help.

    @par rest Accepts del for DELETE
    The \c rest command-line REST client accepts \c del as an alias for the \c DELETE method.

    @par New @@discard Statement
    The new @ref discard_statement "@@discard" statement evaluates an expression and discards its result without a
    @ref return-value-ignored "return-value-ignored" warning.

    @subsection qore_3_0_0_deprecated Deprecated Features
    - <b>integer parse option constants</b>: the \c PO_* integer constants are deprecated in favor of
      @ref Qore::ParseOptions, which holds all 128 option bits; the constants of options beyond bit 63 are \c 0
      and set no option (see @ref qore_3_0_0_compatibility), and later options have no integer constant

    @subsection qore_3_0_0_known_limitations Known Limitations
    - The integrated REPL in the \c qore binary provides line editing via the built-in
      @ref linenoiseintro "linenoise" module (arrow key navigation, command history recall, reverse
      search with Ctrl+R, tab completion). For a full-featured ncurses TUI REPL, see the
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/module-ncurses">module-ncurses</a> \c qrepl application.

