    @subsection qore_3_0_0_bug_fixes Bug Fixes in Qore

    - an HTTP response without a message body whose \c Content-Type was an event stream with a parameter or in
      another case (e.g. \c text/event-stream;charset=utf-8) was sent with \c Content-Length: 0, which ended the
      stream for the client; see @ref Qore::Socket::sendHTTPResponse() "Socket::sendHTTPResponse()" (issue #5758)
    - @ref Qore::reload_module() "reload_module()" of a module that other modules require corrupted the module
      dependency records when the reload failed or when it succeeded, which stopped a debug build with an assertion
      at exit; the records of the module that the reload replaces are now kept until the module is deleted
    - in native AOT code (qcc), a closure in an @ref on_error "on_error", @ref on_exit "on_exit" or
      @ref on_success "on_success" handler that captured a variable the handler declares (a catch or foreach variable)
      and a local of the function made a later read of that local raise LVALUE-ERROR, or read NOTHING; the output
      now matches the AST interpreter
    - qcc (and <tt>qore --compile</tt>) now fails the build when the top-level code of a program cannot be lowered to
      native code, as it does for a function; it wrote an executable that did nothing and exited with status 0
    - the AST interpreter added a @ref timeout_type "timeout" variable to a @ref date_type "date" of no declared type
      (such as the argument of a @ref map "map" expression) as seconds instead of milliseconds with the \c + operator,
      unlike the \c - operator and the IR, JIT and AOT modes; a \c *timeout operand counts as milliseconds too, in every
      mode, with the \c + and \c - operators (a \c softint or \c int operand is still seconds)
    - the signal handlers of a deleted @ref Qore::Program "Program" were released after its data, so an object whose
      last reference a handler's closure held was destroyed without its Program's data, and its destructor could raise
      \c PROGRAM-ERROR; they are now released first (see @ref signal_handler_program_lifetime)
    - the value of a @ref thread_local "thread_local" variable could be read by the code of a
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" created after the Program declaring the variable was deleted, with its type, in
      another thread that ran both Programs' code (the threads of a pool, across sandboxes); the values of the
      threads other than the deleting one were kept until the threads ended, and their destructors could raise
      \c PROGRAM-ERROR; the values of every thread are now released with the variable, and a thread that ends or
      clears its values while the Program is deleted in another thread releases them while the Program can still run
      their destructors (see @ref thread_local_lifetime)
    - a thread that ended made every @ref thread_local "thread_local" variable that a destructor read while it ended
      read-only in every thread ("illegal variable assignment after second phase of variable destruction")
    - evaluating a member initializer of a class or @ref hashdecl "hashdecl" of a deleted
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" (an inherited declaration, or the type of a value used with the
      @ref reflectionintro "reflection" API) crashed; it now raises a \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception (see
      @ref threads_and_program_deletion)
    - a @ref Qore::Reflection::TypedHash "TypedHash", @ref Qore::Reflection::TypedHashMember "TypedHashMember" or
      @ref Qore::Reflection::Type "Type" object read freed memory once the last value of its type was gone and the
      Program that declared it had been deleted; it now keeps the declarations of its type
    - using a @ref lvalue_references "reference" to a lvalue of a deleted @ref Qore::Program "Program", which a
      function of the Program can return, crashed; it now raises a \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception (see
      @ref references_overview)
    - calling a @ref call_reference "call reference" to a function of a deleted @ref Qore::Program "Program", made in
      that Program (for example returned by one of its functions), crashed or returned a wrong value; it now raises a
      \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception, as a call of a reference made in a Program that imported the function does
    - calling a function imported with @ref Qore::Program::importFunction() "Program::importFunction()" after the
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" it was imported from had been deleted used the freed data of that Program and could
      crash; it now raises a \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception, as documented
    - a thread running the code of a @ref Qore::Program "Program" whose deletion waited for it could not call into the
      Program again through the code of another Program (\c PROGRAM-ERROR); see @ref threads_and_program_deletion
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes, leaving the scope of a variable holding a reference to a lvalue that
      was gone, such as a member of a deleted object, raised an exception that the AST interpreter does not raise
    - Qore did not compile with OpenSSL 4, and libqore did not link when the Brotli and LZ4 library directories that
      pkg-config gives were not on the linker's default search path (as with Homebrew on Apple Silicon); libqore now
      links them by their full paths
    - a call whose argument is returned as a reference from another call (ex: <tt>d.m(c.get())</tt>, with
      <tt>reference&lt;int&gt; get()</tt>) could select a variant with a parameter of the referenced type at parse
      time, which does not accept the reference; the variant resolved at run time instead was called without its
      arguments; such a call is now resolved at run time (see @ref overloading)
    - a timed wait of @ref Qore::Thread::Counter "Counter", @ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue",
      @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex", @ref Qore::Thread::RWLock "RWLock" or @ref Qore::Thread::Gate "Gate" woken
      without what it waited for (another waiter took it first, or another %Program's interrupt woke it) waited for
      its whole timeout again; it now waits only for the rest of it
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, a list of selectors with ranges
      (ex: <tt>l[f(), 1..g()]</tt>) iterated by \c foreach or by the @ref map "map", @ref select "select", @ref foldl
      "foldl" or a streaming operator was evaluated as a whole before the first value was used; each selector is now
      evaluated when the values of the previous ones have been used, as with the AST interpreter
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, @ref remove "remove" and @ref delete "delete"
      with a list of selectors whose later selector raised an exception removed nothing; they now remove what the
      earlier selectors selected, as with the AST interpreter
    - iterating a list of selectors with a range (ex: <tt>foreach x in (l[0, 1..-1])</tt>) with negative offsets gave
      other elements than the same expression as a value; it now gives the same elements
    - with the AST interpreter, @ref remove "remove" and @ref delete "delete" with a list of selectors whose later
      selector raised an exception leaked the removed values, and a list of selectors with ranges iterated by
      \c foreach or a functional operator could read a list that the loop had released, which could crash
    - with the AST interpreter, @ref remove "remove" and @ref delete "delete" with square brackets evaluated their
      selectors while they held the lock of a global variable, an object member or a static class variable, so that a
      selector that read it aborted debug builds or deadlocked; the selectors are now evaluated after the path to the
      lvalue and before its lock, as in the other execution modes
    - with the AST interpreter, \c foreach and the @ref op_functional "functional operators" evaluated the indices of
      a list of indices without a range (ex: <tt>l[f(), g()]</tt>), the bounds and the sequence of an empty range, and
      the sequence of a string or binary range twice, and leaked a list of indices; each is now evaluated once
    - a @ref cast "cast" as the lvalue of @ref remove "remove" or @ref delete "delete" (ex: <tt>remove cast<int>(x)</tt>)
      or in the lvalue of a @ref lvalue_references "reference" aborted debug builds or gave wrong results; it is now a
      parse error, as a cast is not storage that can be removed or referred to
    - @ref Qore::Thread::Queue::clear() "Queue::clear()", @ref Qore::Thread::Queue::setError() "Queue::setError()" and
      @ref Qore::TreeMap::put() "TreeMap::put()" released values while holding the container's lock, so a destructor of
      a released value that used the same container could deadlock or break the lock; values are now released after it
    - a separated module required by the path of its <tt>.qm</tt> file
      (<tt>%requires ../lib/ModuleName/ModuleName.qm</tt>) was loaded without its other files, so its classes and
      functions were missing; the whole module is loaded, as with the path of its directory (see
      @ref separated_module_paths)
    - a class whose public member declaration declared both members and static class variables, and its
      subclasses, accepted undeclared members; they are now refused with \c INVALID-MEMBER at runtime, or with a
      parse exception where the parser sees the access, so code that used such members no longer parses (see
      @ref public_members)
    - a reference to an undeclared member of a class with a public member declaration raised two parse exceptions
      when types are required (as with @ref modern "%modern"): the illegal access and the missing type information;
      only the illegal access is now reported
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, a loop condition comparing \c int locals
      one of which is a \c reference<int> local or parameter (ex: <tt>while (i < n)</tt>) kept using the first value
      of the reference when its target changed in the loop; it now reads the reference each time, as with the AST
      interpreter
    - in the IR execution mode, and where the JIT and tiered modes use the IR interpreter, a local variable or
      parameter holding a reference could keep giving an old value after the referenced lvalue changed; it now
      gives the current value, as with the AST interpreter (see @ref lvalue_references)
    - the result of \c *= and \c /= is now the value stored in the lvalue, as for an assignment
      (<tt>x *= y</tt> gives what <tt>x = x * y</tt> gives): with a \c float operand on an lvalue that stores the
      result converted, the computed value was returned instead (ex: <tt>softint v = 7; (v *= 2.5)</tt> gave
      \c 17.5 while \c v holds \c 17, now \c 17), and with an \c int operand on a \c float or \c number
      lvalue with no value the integer computed was returned (ex: <tt>*float f; (f *= 0)</tt> and
      <tt>*float f; (f /= 2)</tt> gave \c 0 while \c f holds \c 0.0, now \c 0.0; <tt>*number n; (n *= 2)</tt> now
      gives \c 0n; <tt>hash<string, float> h = {}; (h.k *= 2)</tt> now gives \c 0.0); in the IR, JIT and tiered
      execution modes and in AOT-compiled code the same happened with a \c number operand on a \c softint or
      \c *softint local or \c thread_local variable
    - the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes run code holding objects faster: loops declaring locals and member
      assignments allocate no memory, and method and constructor calls take fewer references to their receivers and
      arguments, which for objects in recursive graphs saves garbage-collection work
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, an untyped or \c auto local variable holding
      a reference obtained from a container, a call or an argument could give an old value, and an assignment to it
      could be lost; it now reads and writes the referenced lvalue, as with the AST interpreter (see
      @ref lvalue_references)
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, a reference stored as a value (ex: an
      element <tt>h.r</tt> holding <tt>\\x</tt>) is now handled as with the AST interpreter when it is added to an
      empty variable, is the value of an assignment, is an element of a list iterated by \c foreach, or is held by a
      global or \c thread_local variable (see @ref lvalue_references)
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, when an exception left a function, objects
      held by local variables could be destroyed in a different order than with the AST interpreter, and an
      exception raised by a destructor could be lost; local variables are now released last declared first, and the
      value a \c foreach statement iterates when the statement ends, as with the AST interpreter
    - in the JIT and tiered execution modes, the loop variable of a \c foreach statement bound by a closure was
      released only when the function returned, after its \c on_exit and \c on_error handlers, instead of when the
      statement ended
    - in the JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, when \c break left a \c foreach statement
      whose loop variable had been reassigned, the element that the loop variable held before was released after the
      loop variable instead of with the value the statement iterates, before the loop variable, as with the AST
      interpreter
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, \c remove and \c delete on a global,
      \c thread_local or static class variable holding a reference now remove the variable's own value instead of
      the referenced value, as with the AST interpreter (see @ref lvalue_references)
    - in the JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, a value read through a reference held by a local
      variable (ex: <tt>auto v = \\o; v.name();</tt>) is now released at the end of the statement instead of when the
      function returns (see @ref lvalue_references)
    - in the JIT and tiered execution modes, an exception raised by the destructor of the value that the loop
      variable of \c foreach held when an iterator's next element was assigned could crash the program
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, a type error assigning an element to the
      loop variable of \c foreach is now described as a <tt>&lt;foreach lvalue assignment&gt;</tt> error, as with
      the AST interpreter
    - the IR execution mode runs <tt>i += v</tt> on an \c int local with an \c auto local \c v as fast as with an
      \c int local again
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, an operator, a container access or an
      iteration given a reference that arrives as a value (ex: an element <tt>h.r</tt> holding <tt>\\x</tt>) now
      takes the reference as it is, as with the AST interpreter, instead of reading the referenced lvalue (ex:
      <tt>NOTHING + h.r</tt> and <tt>h.r.a</tt> gave the referenced value or its member, <tt>foreach</tt> and
      \c select iterated the referenced list)
    - the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and AOT-compiled code keep the locals of an object type (a class,
      \c object) out of the runtime stack as they keep other locals, with the same garbage-collection behavior: a loop
      assigning an object to such a local runs up to 1.4 times faster
    - a method call whose argument assigns the variable holding the receiver (ex: <tt>o.m(o = new T())</tt>), or a
      call whose argument assigns a variable read for an earlier argument (ex: <tt>f(t, t = x)</tt>), could crash the
      program in some execution modes; the call now runs with the values read before the assignment
    - with the AST interpreter, a call through a variable whose argument assigns it (ex: <tt>c(c = NOTHING)</tt>), and
      <tt>[]</tt>, <tt>{}</tt> or a slice whose index assigns or removes the container's variable (ex:
      <tt>l[(l = x).size() - 1]</tt>), could crash the program; they now use the value that the variable held, as in
      every other execution mode
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, <tt>map $1.key, l</tt> gave \c NOTHING for
      each object in the list instead of its member, and \c map, \c select and \c foldl over an object that is not
      an iterator gave a list of one value instead of the value, as the AST interpreter gives it
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, the objects created for an expression that an
      exception left were destroyed in another order than with the AST interpreter (ex:
      <tt>f(new T("a"), new T("b"), thr())</tt> destroyed \c b first); they are now released in the AST interpreter's
      order
    - in AOT-compiled code, a function called directly from compiled code left an entry on the runtime stack for each
      local variable of its own that is visible to the runtime at each call, so a loop of such calls in top-level code
      took time quadratic in the number of iterations, and memory grew with it
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, the objects created for the sequence of a
      slice, for the source of a functional operator and for the elements of a list or hash literal were destroyed
      when the statement ended (ex: <tt>f(map $1.n, mk(), g())</tt> destroyed them after \c g() ran); they are now
      destroyed when they have been used, as with the AST interpreter
    - in the IR execution mode, the last element of a \c map or \c select over an iterator was destroyed when the
      statement ended, and in every compiled mode an exception that left a fused pipeline of functional operators for
      a \c catch block released the pipeline's source after the block; both are now released as with the AST
      interpreter
    - in the IR execution mode, an exception raised by a destructor while an exception left an expression (ex: when
      the arguments of a call were released) was lost, and a debug build aborted; it is now added to the exception
      raised, as with the AST interpreter
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, the objects created for the arguments of a
      call are now destroyed last argument first when the call returns, as with the AST interpreter, also in calls of
      closures, static methods and constructors and from top-level code, and for an argument given by an expression
      such as <tt>c ? new T() : new T()</tt>
    - in AOT-compiled code, an object that a local variable held when it was assigned another value in a loop was
      destroyed only at the next assignment or when the function returned instead of at the assignment
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, assigning an element of a local hash or
      list whose key or index expression assigns the variable (ex: <tt>h{(h = x).firstKey()} = v</tt>) stored into
      the container the variable held before, and could crash the program in the IR execution mode; it now stores into
      the container assigned, as with the AST interpreter
    - the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and AOT-compiled code mark an object held by a local of no type
      (\c auto) as reachable from the stack, as they mark one held by a local of an object type
    - in the IR execution mode and wherever the JIT and tiered execution modes run a function with the IR
      interpreter, the first call of a large function in a thread with a pending cancellation (ex: in code that
      caught a \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception) raised the exception before the function ran; it is now raised at the
      program's next cancellation check point, as with the AST interpreter
    - in the JIT and tiered execution modes, a local variable of no reference type (\c auto, \c any or no type)
      assigned a reference (ex: <tt>auto r = \\x;</tt>) kept reading as the value of the referenced lvalue when it
      was assigned after the lvalue changed (ex: <tt>int x = 2; auto r = \\x; x = 10;</tt> then \c r read as \c 2);
      it now reads as the referenced lvalue, as with the AST interpreter
    - in AOT-compiled code, a class with a public member declaration but no public members, and its subclasses,
      accepted undeclared members; they are now refused with \c INVALID-MEMBER, as in the other execution modes;
      modules compiled before this release keep the old behavior until they are recompiled (see
      @ref public_members)
    - in the IR execution mode, and where the JIT and tiered execution modes ran a function with the IR interpreter,
      <tt>i += v</tt> on an \c int or \c softint local \c i changed the value of a local \c v to an integer (ex: a
      \c string local holding \c "3" then held \c 3, and an \c int local with no value held \c 0), and so did a
      loop condition <tt>i < n</tt> on \c int locals with no value
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, \c += on an \c auto lvalue with no value
      or holding \c NULL lost the complex type of a hash or list operand (ex: <tt>auto v; v += <StatInfo>{};</tt>
      gave a plain \c hash), and so did Mapper::mapData() with a hashdecl input value; the lvalue now holds the
      operand with its type, as with the AST interpreter (\c hash<StatInfo> in the example)
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, the result of \c += and \c -= on a variable
      could be a value that the variable's type does not store (ex: <tt>any v; (v += <StatInfo>{}).fullType()</tt>
      gave \c "hash<StatInfo>" while \c v holds a plain \c hash); the result is now the value the variable holds, as
      with the AST interpreter
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes, a closure of an @ref Qore::Expression "Expression" or a
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" created after another one had been deleted could run the freed closure's code,
      giving wrong results, exceptions or a crash; a closure now runs its own code (see @ref closure)
    - in the IR execution mode, and where the JIT and tiered modes use the IR interpreter, a \c thread_local variable
      read after an assignment or an in-place change in the same function could give a value it does not hold, such
      as the unconverted value assigned or its value before the change; the variable's value is now given, as with
      the AST interpreter (see @ref thread_local_variables)
    - in the IR, JIT and tiered execution modes and in AOT-compiled code, \c ++ and \c -- on a value of a hash typed
      with float or number values (ex: <tt>hash<string, float> h = {"v": 2.5}; ++h.v</tt>) gave an integer and
      stored a truncated value; they now give the same results as the AST interpreter (\c 3.5 in the example)
    - an AOT-compiled module loaded with a module older than the one it was compiled against, which lacks an enum or
      an enum member that the module uses, failed with only "unsupported AOT slot metadata"; the error now names the
      missing symbol and the modules loaded
    - a cast in an AOT-compiled module to a class that is not declared where the module is loaded, as when it is
      loaded with a module older than the one it was compiled against, let the value through unchecked; it now raises
      an \c IR-CAST-ERROR naming the type
    - fixed a deadlock when a user module failed to load while another thread used a binary module, such as the jni
      module, whose program cleanup code calls the module manager: the failed module is now destroyed without the
      module manager's lock, which every thread loading a module afterwards also waited for
    - a reference cycle through the thread initialization code of a Program (@ref Qore::Program::setThreadInit() and
      @ref Qore::set_thread_init()), such as code that refers to the Program object, was never collected, so the
      Program and the values the code refers to were leaked; such a cycle is now collected like any other
    - the C++ API function \c BinaryNode::writeTo() did not extend the size of the value when it wrote past its end,
      so the data written there was not part of the value; it now does, and fills a gap with zero bytes
    - an argument for a parameter declared as \c reference<T> whose target could not be resolved, as with
      \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED in a recursion that passes on the reference, raised a second \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR
      naming two identical types after the actual error; only the actual error is raised now, in all execution modes
    - an argument for a parameter declared as \c reference<T> that refers to a member that an object does not have
      yet, of a class that declares no members, crashed the process
    - resolving a long chain of references, as made by a recursion that passes on a reference, could overflow the
      stack and crash the process instead of raising \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED, seen in JIT and AOT code on Linux
    - %Qore and modules built with its CMake macros now link executables, libraries and binary modules to a temporary
      file that is renamed into place, so a build killed during a link no longer leaves a truncated binary that the
      next build treats as up to date
    - an AOT module refused by the restrictions of the @ref Qore::Program "Program" loading it now raises
      \c AOT-MODULE-RESTRICTED instead of the generic \c MODULE-LOAD-ERROR (see @ref module_parse_option_masks)
    - when an AOT module could not be loaded (for example, refused by the restrictions of the
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" loading it) and its source module failed as well, only the source error was
      reported; the AOT module's error now follows it in the exception chain (see @ref aot_module_source_fallback)
    - \c BINARY-MODULE-SOURCE-FALLBACK and the @ref user_module_children "child module" warnings of a module loaded
      by \c %%requires or \c %%try-module are now parse warnings at the directive, returned by Program::parse(),
      instead of being written to stderr; a broken child is still always reported (see
      @ref aot_module_source_fallback)
    - when an AOT module of a build tree was out of date with its sources and its source module failed to load, only
      the source error was reported; an \c AOT-MODULE-STALE error now follows it in the exception chain
    - an AOT module in a build tree that is a regular file with the dependency file written by \c qcc next to it is
      now checked against its sources like a linked module, so an out-of-date module is no longer used; installed
      modules are not checked (see @ref aot_module_build_tree_staleness)
    - an AOT module of a build tree was not found out of date, nor compiled again by an incremental build, when a file
      that it includes with \c %%include, or a module that one of its dependencies requires or re-exports, changed:
      \c qcc now lists them in the module's dependency file (see @ref aot_module_build_tree_staleness)
    - an AOT module of a build tree was loaded when a module it requires (directly or not, or re-exported) was loaded
      from another file than the one it was compiled against, such as a module that appeared in a directory of the
      module path that is searched first; it is now out of date (see @ref aot_module_build_tree_staleness)
    - a @ref thread_local "thread_local" variable declared in a user module was refused when the module inherited
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL": thread-local storage is not thread control (see
      @ref module_parse_option_masks)
    - @ref Qore::set_local_var_value() "set_local_var_value()" assigns a value as an assignment does: a value was
      converted to the type of the value holder, and a read-only variable was changed
    - a debugger's pause request (@ref Qore::DebugProgram::breakProgram() "breakProgram()",
      @ref Qore::DebugProgram::breakProgramThread() "breakProgramThread()") skipped threads running module code or a
      debugger callback, so a thread logging in a loop could not be paused
    - \c qdbg ends the program and exits with 128 plus the signal number on \c SIGINT or \c SIGTERM, reports the end of
      the threads of a program it kills, and waits for the program before processing the next command of a script on
      its input; \c qdbg-remote no longer loads all connections for a URL without a scheme (see @ref debugging_qdbg)
    - a call passing no value to an overloaded function with three or more variants accepting no value equally well
      could select a different variant at parse time than at run time (see @ref overloading_no_value)
    - at process exit, modules were deleted before the modules that require them, and objects held through opaque
      references were released too late, so destructors failed with \c PROGRAM-ERROR (see
      @ref module_deletion_order)
    - an unhandled exception raised by a destructor at program exit leaves exit status 3 (see @ref exception_handling)
    - a debugger callback could get a deleted @ref Qore::ProgramControl "ProgramControl" object, which could crash the
      process, and a copy of a @ref Qore::Breakpoint "Breakpoint" shared the original's ID and Program (see
      @ref programcontrol_object_identity and @ref Qore::Breakpoint::copy() "Breakpoint::copy()")
    - a breakpoint set with a file name other than the one the file was parsed with, such as an absolute path, found
      no statement (see @ref debugging_breakpoints;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5558">issue 5558</a>)
    - fixed messages formatted with mismatched arguments: a <tt>%%s</tt> in the error text of a failed WebSocket
      upgrade or of a value thrown by native code crashed the process, as did
      @ref Qore::Program::importFunction() "Program::importFunction()" with an empty new name, and some errors showed
      wrong values (dates, proxy URLs, 64-bit integers)
    - the local variables of a frame stopped in a debugger (@ref Qore::get_local_vars() "get_local_vars()") included
      the runtime's temporary \c ref_arg_helper variables
    - an @ref Qore::Expression "Expression" could instantiate a class with unimplemented abstract methods instead of
      raising \c ABSTRACT-CLASS-ERROR
    - an untyped list or hash, as built by language bindings, was rejected for a typed container parameter in runtime
      variant matching, and typed containers needing a conversion were ranked as exact matches (see
      @ref overloading_untyped_containers;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5541">issue 5541</a>)
    - a class added at runtime with <tt>QoreNamespace::addSystemClass()</tt> did not initialize its own or its
      user-class parents' members (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5544">issue 5544</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5554">issue 5554</a>)
    - a \c QoreRecursiveThreadLock created outside \c qore_init() / \c qore_cleanup(), or in a child forked then, was
      not recursive
    - serializing a weak reference to a destroyed object raised \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED (see
      @ref serializable_weak_references)
    - deleting a @ref Qore::Program "Program" while the current thread ran its code crashed, and could deadlock with a
      background thread (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5538">issue 5538</a>)
    - C++ code creating a hash of a hashdecl with member initializers without an \c ExceptionSink crashed
    - zoneinfo files are read with their 64-bit data and footer rule (RFC 9636), fixing "slim" files, times after 2037
      and many times before 1970 (see @ref zoneinfo_files)
    - @ref Qore::date(string) "date(string)" and @ref Qore::TimeZone::date(string) "TimeZone::date(string)" read a
      zone designator after a time without seconds, a lower-case \c z and a decimal comma (see
      @ref date_string_conversion)
    - @ref Qore::Socket::shutdown() "Socket::shutdown()" sends the TLS \c close_notify alert on a secure connection
    - server-sent events are parsed as the WHATWG HTML event stream format requires (comments, repeated fields, empty
      IDs and data lines, a leading byte order mark, blocks without fields; see
      @ref Qore::Socket::readServerSentEvent() "Socket::readServerSentEvent()")
    - fixed heap corruption when threads used the process environment at the same time: %Qore serializes all its
      access to the environment, which binary modules can join with \c QoreEnvironmentReadLock and
      \c QoreEnvironmentValue
    - fixed child processes of @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" hanging when other threads ran in the parent; binary modules
      register fork handlers for their own locks with \c qore_register_fork_handler(), and can make a child end with
      \c _exit() with \c qore_fork_child_exit_quickly()
    - a child process of @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" closed or used its parent's database sessions (see
      @ref datasource_fork)
    - an exception assimilated from an externally managed \c ExceptionSink was counted on the wrong thread
    - fixed a deadlock when the thread initialization code (@ref Qore::set_thread_init() "set_thread_init()") started a
      thread or read the call stacks of all threads
    - waits on named pipes on macOS did not see the end of the pipe, and waited for their whole timeout
    - the ends of a pipe from @ref Qore::File::getPipe() "File::getPipe()" were never closed
    - reads and writes of a @ref Qore::File "File" or the standard output on a nonblocking descriptor failed, returned
      no value or dropped output; they wait for data or room (see @ref Qore::ReadOnlyFile "ReadOnlyFile")
    - binary integers read from a pipe in parts were read wrongly, and
      @ref Qore::File::writei8() "File::writei8()" and @ref Qore::File::writei8LSB() "File::writei8LSB()" wrote only
      4 bytes
    - @ref Qore::FileInputStream::peek() "FileInputStream::peek()" on a pipe, terminal or socket lost the byte peeked
      and ignored the timeout
    - writes of 2 GiB or more with @ref Qore::File "File" and @ref Qore::FileOutputStream "FileOutputStream" returned
      wrong counts or failed, also on macOS
    - empty binary values were stored as SQL \c NULL by the \c pgsql and \c sqlite3 drivers
    - the files of a separated user module other than its <tt>.qm</tt> file were parsed with the warnings of the code
      loading the module (see @ref user_modules)
    - fixed a memory leak when a binary module could not be loaded because a namespace it declares already exists
    - @ref Qore::sprintf() "sprintf()" and related functions are no longer constant functions, as they can raise
      exceptions
    - in-place arithmetic on @ref number "numbers" raised their precision without limit and crashed (see
      @ref number_max_precision)
    - @ref Qore::qore_add_module_to_blacklist() "qore_add_module_to_blacklist()" and
      @ref Qore::qore_add_module_to_blacklist_ex() "qore_add_module_to_blacklist_ex()" raise \c BLACKLIST-ERROR for a
      name with a NUL character
    - strings with a NUL character are formatted whole with \c "%y" and \c "%N" (see @ref string_formatting); for the
      fixes to the @ref jsonintro "json" module (NUL characters, JSON-RPC errors and versions), see @ref json_v3_0_0
    - fixed a crash in @ref Qore::ReadOnlyFile::startPollRead() "ReadOnlyFile::startPollRead()" with a path that
      cannot be converted to the default encoding
    - a closed @ref Qore::File "File" returned the number of the descriptor it closed from
      @ref Qore::AbstractPollableIoObjectBase::getPollableDescriptor() "getPollableDescriptor()", and
      \c QoreFile::detachFd() left the object able to close the detached descriptor
    - fixed a crash when a binary module added to a namespace of a running Program while another thread imported a
      user module from it, as with the \c jni module
    - fixed a crash of @ref Qore::encode_url() "encode_url()" and
      @ref Qore::encode_uri_request() "encode_uri_request()" with truncated UTF-8 input
    - fixed @ref background "background" expressions: @ref implicit_arguments "implicit arguments" and the arguments
      of calls in @ref map "map", @ref select "select", @ref foldl "foldl" and @ref foldr "foldr" expressions had no
      value in the new thread, and modifying a member of the object is a parse error
    - fixed crashes, leaks and unhandled destructor exceptions of @ref background "background" when preparing its
      expression failed, its thread could not be created, or it released the last reference to an object
    - a @ref list_assignment "list assignment" used as an expression had the type of the last element of its
      right-hand side that was parsed (no type when the right-hand side had fewer elements than the left-hand side, or
      was not a list literal) instead of the type of its right-hand side, so that its value was refused where a type is
      checked, as in a typed hash initializer
    - fixed @ref background "background" expressions that use a local variable in the arguments of a
      @ref new "new" expression or in the object of an object method reference (such as
      <tt><b>background</b> f(<b>new</b> C(x))</tt> or <tt><b>background</b> (\\o.m())()</tt>): the thread raised
      \c LVALUE-ERROR or used no value for the variable instead of the value it had when the thread started; with the
      IR, JIT, tiered and AOT execution modes this happened with <tt>new hash<K, V>(...)</tt> and
      <tt>new list<T>(...)</tt>
    - modifying a local variable in an argument of a call in a @ref background "background" expression crashed the
      AST interpreter and modified a copy in the other execution modes; it is now a parse error, as elsewhere in the
      expression
    - modifying an element or member of a local variable or member in a @ref background "background" expression crashed
      the AST interpreter, and \c /=, \c -- of the AST interpreter's background copies were \c *= and \c ++; a value that
      the thread copies is now refused at parse time, a member of an object is modified in the object in every execution
      mode, and a value of unknown type is refused
    - the smallest integer divided by \c -1 (@ref division_operator "/", @ref modulo_operator "%") crashed with
      \c SIGFPE
    - methods implemented by @ref Qore::SQL::AbstractDatasource "AbstractDatasource",
      @ref Qore::SQL::AbstractSQLStatement "AbstractSQLStatement",
      @ref Qore::AbstractLineIterator "AbstractLineIterator" and
      @ref Qore::AbstractPollOperation "AbstractPollOperation" raised \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED on a subclass
      implemented in %Qore
    - the top-level code parsed by @ref Qore::Program::parse() "Program::parse()" was parsed in the class
      @ref Qore::Program "Program", so a bare call could call one of its static methods
    - fixed a data race in @ref Qore::Socket::isOpen() "Socket::isOpen()" while another thread closed the socket
    - URL errors no longer contain the URL's password; see @ref Qore::get_masked_url() "get_masked_url()"
    - text in encodings that are not compatible with ASCII (UTF-16, EBCDIC) is processed correctly by
      @ref Qore::sprintf() "sprintf()", @ref Qore::ReadOnlyFile::readLine() "ReadOnlyFile::readLine()", line
      iterators, number formatting, @ref Qore::html_encode() "html_encode()", @ref Qore::encode_url() "encode_url()"
      and related functions (see @ref known_encodings)
    - @ref Qore::encode_uri_request() "encode_uri_request()" encodes the characters of a string in UTF-8, not its
      bytes, and @ref Qore::strmul() "strmul()" keeps the encoding of its string
    - @ref Qore::gzip() "gzip()" and @ref Qore::bzip2() "bzip2()" raise their documented errors for invalid negative
      levels
    - fixed \c QoreString::concat() of a string's first characters overrunning buffers and \c QoreString::compareSoft()
      for prefixes and empty strings in the C++ API
    - fixed data races when a Program parsed classes that other threads used, such as the classes of a binary module
      loaded with @ref Qore::load_module() "load_module()" or of a module that other threads ran
    - deeply nested values and code no longer exhaust the stack: closure chains and nested code are released
      iteratively, and evaluating, serializing, comparing and formatting deeply nested expressions and data raise
      \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED
    - a thread faulting with \c SIGBUS, \c SIGILL, \c SIGFPE or \c SIGTRAP hung instead of terminating the process
    - UTC offsets such as \c "+01:00" were refused as a DBI driver's \c timezone option
    - fixed @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource" and @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool "DatasourcePool" connection
      leaks when an option fails on open, a transaction lock left held by
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::close() "close()" and @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::reset() "reset()", and refused
      values kept by @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::setOption() "Datasource::setOption()"
    - fixed data races on the transaction lock state, autocommit flag, timeouts and
      @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool::copy() "DatasourcePool::copy()" of datasources
    - an @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" is closed by the thread using its connection, its actions are
      serialized, it is closed with its datasource, and closing or destroying it no longer opens a connection
    - fixed @ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue" garbage collection: cycles through a queue or its copy were not
      collected, objects of a cycle could be deleted after a value was taken from a queue or a
      @ref Qore::TreeMap "TreeMap", and a queue was scanned for life after holding a container
    - fixed a crash of \c QoreQueueHelper::clear(), a reference leak of \c QoreQueueHelper and a data race in
      @ref Qore::Thread::Queue::size() "Queue::size()" and related methods
    - thread stack sizes: on glibc, a thread's stack includes its thread-local storage,
      @ref Qore::set_default_thread_stack_size() "set_default_thread_stack_size()" refuses a size not larger than
      the stack guard, and the primary thread's limit set by a runtime such as the JVM is derived from its real stack
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5518">issue 5518</a>)
    - a closure releases its captured variables in the order they were declared, not by address
    - malformed UTF-8, UTF-16 and UTF-32 input raises \c ENCODING-CONVERSION-ERROR on every platform, also through
      streams and with the macOS iconv
    - @ref Qore::system() "system()" starts commands with \c posix_spawn() where available and raises
      \c SYSTEM-ERROR when it cannot start one
    - hash keys in another encoding than UTF-8 are converted to UTF-8 (see @ref hash_key_encoding)
    - lines read from generic \c "UTF-16" data with a little-endian byte order mark were garbled, and conversion
      streams returned the byte order mark as a character (see @ref utf16_in_qore)
    - strings in UTF-16 converted to wrong numbers, booleans and dates
    - conversions to stateful encodings such as \c "UTF-7" and \c "ISO-2022-JP" lost escape sequences and the final
      reset bytes
    - strings in multi-byte encodings such as \c "EUC-JP", \c "SHIFT_JIS" and \c "GBK" were handled one byte per
      character (see @ref known_encodings and @ref qore_3_0_0_compatibility)
    - a default character encoding that is not compatible with ASCII is refused (see @ref default_encoding)
    - encodings created from their name, such as \c "UTF-32", \c "UCS-2LE" and EBCDIC code pages, were handled as
      single-byte ASCII-compatible encodings (see @ref known_encodings)
    - \c peek() of input streams returned bytes 0x80 - 0xff as negative values
    - converting a string to an encoding that needs more bytes took time quadratic in its length
    - binary module metadata returned through \c QoreModuleInfo::info is released when the module is destroyed
    - TLS was unusable when the OpenSSL configuration file could not be processed (see @ref openssl_config)
    - fixed an exception rethrown by an \c on_error handler being lost when another \c on_error handler of the same
      block ran after it, so that the function returned normally
    - fixed @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool "DatasourcePool" memory corruption when a pool was deleted while another
      thread was in one of its methods, and a reference counting error for its event queue
    - fixed @ref Qore::parse_url() "parse_url()" returning a wrong host and port for a malformed IPv6 address; it
      raises \c PARSE-URL-ERROR instead
    - fixed @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" delivering a response body that it streams to an output stream or a
      callback still compressed; see @ref httpclient_response_body
    - fixed @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" and @ref httpclientiointro "HttpClientIo" raising a send error
      instead of returning an HTTP/1.1 response sent before the request was read, such as a \c 413, when the server
      then closes the connection, also over HTTPS and for streamed request bodies; see @ref httpclient_early_response
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5630">issue 5630</a>)
    - @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" repeats an idempotent request on a new connection when the server closed a
      reused keep-alive connection before it answered, also when the request write failed, and for any \c 408 on a
      reused connection, and raises \c HTTP1-CONNECTION-CLOSED for a request that is not idempotent; a \c 408 on a
      reused connection is not returned as the answer to the request
    - a TLS @ref Qore::Socket "Socket" stays open when a write fails because the peer closed the connection, so the
      data the peer sent before can still be read, and \c SOCKET-SSL-ERROR for a network failure has the error number
      as its argument; such a connection is closed without a TLS \c close_notify alert
    - fixed \c qget: \c ftp:// and \c ftps:// downloads, saving an empty body, the exit status and error output of a
      failed retrieval, and a \c Content-Disposition file name that could write outside the current directory
    - fixed a crash when a file included with @ref include "%include" ends inside a literal or a block comment
    - fixed \c getEncoding() of @ref Qore::StringInputStream "StringInputStream",
      @ref Qore::StringOutputStream "StringOutputStream", @ref Qore::FileOutputStream "FileOutputStream" and
      @ref Qore::InputStreamLineIterator "InputStreamLineIterator" returning an empty string, and
      @ref Qore::StringOutputStream::getData() "StringOutputStream::getData()" ignoring the constructor's encoding
    - fixed @ref Qore::TransformOutputStream "TransformOutputStream" and
      @ref Qore::TransformInputStream "TransformInputStream" holding back decompressed output that exceeds one buffer
    - fixed @ref Qore::Socket::readServerSentEvent() "Socket::readServerSentEvent()" with a content encoding
      returning only the first event of a compressed stream and rejecting the \c deflate, \c x-deflate, \c x-gzip and
      \c x-bzip2 HTTP content codings
    - fixed @ref Qore::bunzip2_to_binary() "bunzip2_to_binary()" and
      @ref Qore::bunzip2_to_string() "bunzip2_to_string()" allocating gigabytes for truncated input
    - fixed an exception raised by the default value expression of an omitted argument being reported as unhandled
      instead of being raised to the caller
    - fixed a crash when a thread exited while another thread deleted a @ref Qore::Program "Program" it had run code in
    - fixed an unqualified call reference to a private static method (<tt>\\check()</tt>) raising
      \c RUNTIME-OVERLOAD-ERROR when called from outside the class
    - <b>security</b>: fixed @ref Qore::create_object() "create_object()",
      @ref Qore::create_object_args() "create_object_args()" and the object-creating methods of the
      @ref reflectionintro "reflection" module checking a class from a domain that a Program must allow explicitly
      (such as a @ref Qore::DebugProgram "DebugProgram" subclass) the wrong way round, which bypassed sandboxing
      restrictions, and running the constructor of a refused class; the new \c QoreClass::runtimeCheckDomain() C++
      API makes the same check
    - fixed \c return in a @ref foreach "foreach" loop over a reference truncating the referenced list; see
      @ref foreach_reference_exit
    - fixed @ref Qore::CFLAGS omitting the macOS deployment target of the library
    - fixed derived types such as \c list<hash<X>> being taken from a destroyed type whose memory was reused
    - fixed crashes from the \c Program::import*() methods, including
      @ref Qore::Program::importSystemApi() "Program::importSystemApi()", and from parsing or loading a module into a
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" while other threads ran code in it or looked up its names
    - fixed stream sockets bound on all interfaces being unreachable over IPv6 (see @ref qore_3_0_0_compatibility),
      and @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()" trying only the first resolved address
    - fixed Base64 conversion of an empty binary leaving an unterminated string buffer
    - fixed @ref Qore::Serializable "Serializable" deserialization losing references to containers deserialized
      after the value referring to them, and serialization referring to the wrong container when a released
      container's memory was reused
    - <b>security</b>: fixed @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" redirect handling: relative \c Location values are
      resolved per RFC 3986, credentials and cookies are not sent to another origin, a redirect from a network server
      to a UNIX socket or to an invalid host is refused, and response codes and methods follow the HTTP
      specification; see @ref httpclient_redirects
    - fixed crashes when parsing, running or freeing deeply nested code, and when freeing deeply nested lists and
      hashes (see @ref qore_3_0_0_compatibility)
    - fixed @ref garbage_collection "deterministic garbage collection" destroying objects of a cycle that were still
      referenced from outside it, also when the member closing the cycle was replaced, and leaving the reference
      count of a cycle wrong after @ref remove "remove" or @ref delete "delete" of an object member
    - fixed @ref garbage_collection "deterministic garbage collection" never collecting some cycles: a closure and an
      object it captures, a cycle holding a container also referenced from outside it, and a cycle whose blocking
      references went away
    - fixed livelocks and a deadlock of recursive reference scans when several threads dereference objects of one
      large recursive set at the same time, which could stop an HTTP server from answering requests
    - fixed the scalability of method calls and of member, global, closure-bound and by-reference variable access
      from many threads, which serialized on per-object or process-wide locks, and redundant recursive reference
      scans, including whole-graph scans by @ref remove "remove" and @ref delete "delete" of unrelated values
    - fixed get_all_thread_call_stacks() reading incomplete or deleted call stacks of other threads, which could
      crash the debugger
    - fixed get_local_vars() releasing a reference it did not own for a local variable holding a weak reference
    - fixed qore_cleanup() not waiting for background threads to finish their teardown
    - fixed a weak reference (<tt>:=</tt>) to a list or hash whose last strong reference was released reading or
      freeing it again; such a list or hash reads as empty
    - fixed crashes when scanning or destroying long chains or cycles of objects or copying long exception chains
    - fixed a memory leak of the events that a @ref Qore::Socket "Socket", @ref Qore::File "File" or
      @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" posts to an @ref event_handling "event queue" that has been deleted
    - fixed a crash after the parse error for a \c my, \c our or \c thread_local list declaration whose first
      element is not a variable
    - fixed @ref Qore::Reflection::Type::getOrNothingType() "Type::getOrNothingType()" and
      @ref Qore::Reflection::Type::getMandatoryType() "Type::getMandatoryType()" for soft list types, and soft list
      conversions of initialized members and default arguments
    - fixed an assignment that a typed container rejects leaving an empty key, element or path behind
    - fixed class member and static variable initializers and default argument values bypassing their declared types
    - fixed parallel documentation builds of the binary modules bundled with Qore reading unfinished core tags
    - fixed the initializer of a constant being evaluated twice when another constant referred to it first
    - fixed string-to-float conversion returning an uninitialized value for empty input and giving
      platform-dependent results; see @ref string_to_float_conversion

    - @ref dataproviderintro "DataProvider" module: fixed static initializers registered after a failing one never
      being run, types restored from their descriptions, generated examples that a field rejects, and soft boolean and
      soft list validation; see @ref dataprovider_v3_11 for all changes

    - \c qdp and the @ref providerindexintro "ProviderIndex" and @ref providerindexutilintro "ProviderIndexUtil"
      modules
      - fixed \c qdp exiting without shutting down data provider services, which left external processes orphaned
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5383">issue 5383</a>)
      - fixed the index directory being created untraversable, and an interrupted or failed index creation leaving no
        index; an app or action that cannot be initialized no longer fails the index build
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5383">issue 5383</a>)
      - see @ref providerindex_v1_4 and @ref providerindexutil_v1_2 for all changes

    - Core language
      - fixed potential crashes when a running @ref Qore::Program "Program" loads a module while other threads
        resolve names in it
      - fixed the thread stack guard being reduced or disabled in processes with a large thread-local storage area,
        so that runaway recursion crashed instead of raising \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED
      - <b>security</b>: fixed builtin class methods running in the context of the wrong
        @ref Qore::Program "Program", so that C++ checks of the caller's restrictions such as
        @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "PO_NO_FILESYSTEM" were not enforced
      - fixed DBI drivers being unable to register a \c QDBI_METHOD_DESCRIBE method in a debug build
      - @ref Qore::system() "system()", @ref Qore::backquote() "backquote()" and the
        @ref backquote_operator "backquote operator" restore an unblocked signal mask in child processes
      - fixed bareword resolution in constructors and class scopes
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/40">issue 40</a>) and calls to member closures
        defined as class fields (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/39">issue 39</a>)
      - fixed non-blocking file and socket poll reads, TCP connections whose readiness probe is interrupted by a
        signal, and synchronous reads of @ref Qore::ReadOnlyFile "ReadOnlyFile" and
        @ref Qore::FileInputStream "FileInputStream" on descriptors opened with \c O_NONBLOCK
      - fixed closures created in object scope with the @ref background "background operator" running with a
        stale captured object
      - fixed false-positive and missing @ref instanceof "instanceof" \c always-returns-False parse warnings
      - fixed regular expression matching never completing on subjects larger than 5,000,000 bytes and reporting
        "no match" when PCRE2 gives up; global matching, substitution and splitting are interrupt points; see
        @ref qore_regex_limits and @ref qore_3_0_0_regex_errors

    - fixed a bug handling nested conditional parsing directives
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4999">issue 4999</a>)

    - fixed a memory leak in the @ref Qore::TreeMap "TreeMap" class with objects that create circular references
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5028">issue 5028</a>)

    - fixed a potential crash when raising parse warnings with no associated @ref Qore::Program "Program" object

    - fixed empty hash and list literals not being accepted as function arguments of complex hash and list types
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/2647">issue 2647</a>)

    - fixed parsing of namespaced typed hash literals like \c <Qore::StatInfo>{}
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5147">issue 5147</a>)

    - fixed a crash, or a leaked open connection, when a thread holding a
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource" connection exited after the Datasource object was destroyed

    - @ref reflectionintro "reflection" module
      - @ref Qore::Reflection::Class::getConstant() "Class::getConstant()" searches the class hierarchy
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4998">issue 4998</a>)
      - fixed @ref Qore::Reflection::Class::getStaticVariants() "Class::getStaticVariants()" and
        @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractClass::getVariants() "AbstractClass::getVariants()" omitting static methods

    - @ref restclientintro "RestClient" module
      - fixed non-blocking polling with connections that use basic auth
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5001">issue 5001</a>)
      - see @ref restclientv2_7 for all changes

    - @ref restschemavalidatorintro "RestSchemaValidator", @ref openapi3intro "OpenApi3", and
      @ref swaggerintro "Swagger" modules
      - fixed validating a value holding @ref NULL "NULL" raising \c RUNTIME-OVERLOAD-ERROR
      - fixed a described schema reference getting a copy of the referenced type that did not apply all of its rules,
        and parameters sharing a name and a schema sharing their documentation
      - see @ref restschemavalidator_2_5 and @ref swagger_2_6 for all changes

    - @ref resthandlerintro "RestHandler" module
      - fixed handling \c OPTIONS requests with an \c action parameter
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4988">issue 4988</a>); see @ref rh_1_6_7

    - @ref utilintro "Util" module
      - fixed the @ref Util::glob_to_regex() "glob_to_regex()" function
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4996">issue 4996</a>)
    - \c qpp writes its output files to relative paths correctly and reports an error for a hashdecl member without
      a <tt>//!</tt> comment and for unexpected text after the comment of a function or constant group
    - fixed a deadlock when a @ref Qore::Program "Program" was created from a Program in which a binary module such
      as \c python keeps data while another thread used the module
    - a value whose return is interrupted by an exception that a \c catch block handles is not returned; see
      @ref return_interrupted_by_exception
    - Compound assignments, \c ++ and \c -- convert their result to the declared type of the lvalue or raise
      \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR, \c += and \c -= on an or-nothing lvalue with no value start from the plain type's
      default value, and \c *= and \c /= on members and elements use their value; see
      @ref compound_assignment_conversion
    - fixed the @ref unshift "unshift" operator reading freed memory when the list's element type converted the value
    - fixed calling a pseudo-method through a @ref weak_assignment_operator "weak reference" to a destroyed object
      instead of raising \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5509">issue 5509</a>)
    - fixed @ref Qore::seconds() "seconds()" and other relative-date conversions for durations of more than 2^31
      seconds (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5507">issue 5507</a>)
    - fixed @ref relative_dates "relative dates" whose components have opposite signs not being normalized
    - fixed a \c number smaller than about \c 1e-17 in magnitude converting to the string \c 0
    - <b>security</b>: fixed @ref string_formatting "string formatting" functions such as
      @ref Qore::sprintf() "sprintf()" writing past a stack buffer for long field widths and precisions; values
      beyond the documented limits raise \c SPRINTF-ERROR
    - fixed splitting quoted fields with @ref Qore::AbstractLineIterator::getSplitLine() "getSplitLine()" and
      @ref Qore::zzz8stringzzz9::split() "<string>::split()"; the new \c quote_escape parameter selects RFC 4180
      splitting
    - fixed reading lines in encodings that are not ASCII-compatible, such as UTF-16, ending only at <tt>&quot;\\n&quot;</tt>
    - the files of a @ref user_modules "user module" split into a directory are parsed in a fixed order (see
      @ref mod_overview), and @ref Qore::get_module_hash() "get_module_hash()" reports the right file name for one
      found through a relative module path
    - fixed @ref Qore::call_object_method() "call_object_method()",
      @ref Qore::call_static_method() "call_static_method()" and the \c hasCallable*Method() methods waiting for, or
      deadlocking with, a module load
    - fixed hangs and crashes of @ref signal_handling "signal handling" when the signal thread's wakeup was denied,
      when several threads changed signal handlers at once, at exit, and when a signal callback's handler was
      replaced while it ran
    - fixed signals without a handler not ending the process on macOS, as \c SIGUSR1 or \c SIGTERM after
      @ref Qore::remove_signal_handler() "remove_signal_handler()"; a signal is now processed after its handler is
      removed as if it never had one, on all platforms
    - fixed @ref Qore::SSLCertificate "SSLCertificate" validity dates being read in the local time zone and with
      wrong years; an invalid date raises \c SSLCERTIFICATE-DATE-ERROR
    - fixed the serialization of @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource" and
      @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool "DatasourcePool" losing driver option types and splitting option values; data
      serialized by %Qore 3.0 cannot be deserialized by earlier versions
    - fixed \c Datasource configuration and option methods releasing the transaction lock of an open transaction
    - fixed the \c rest command sending a URL fragment in the request path, exiting with status 0 after a failed
      request, and not resolving an endpoint path against the URL of a schema
    - fixed the C++ API \c QoreHashNode::getKeyValueExistence() leaving \c exists unset when it raises an exception,
      and \c DateTime::add() ignoring its argument for an absolute date
    - fixed building %Qore from a <tt>git archive</tt> source archive (see \c BUILDING), recording the lexer source
      path in debug information, and building against OpenSSL without engine support (ex: EL10)
    - the binary modules built with %Qore export only their entry points and public APIs, like external modules
    - fixed the documentation build of external modules: the shared HTML template files outside the %Qore prefix,
      \c qore_wrap_dox() leaving an empty output file after a failed conversion, \c qore_external_user_module(), and
      build directories with spaces
    - an HTTP/1.0 connection without an explicit \c keep-alive was kept open for reuse; HTTP/1 connection reuse
      follows RFC 9112 (see @ref httpclient_persistence)
    - the \c pre_encoded_urls option of @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" refused the unreserved character
      <tt>~</tt> in request paths
    - the HTTP version and path of an HTTP/1 request that followed a UTF-16 body on a persistent connection were read
      in the encoding of that body instead of the header encoding
    - <tt>toString(NF_Raw)</tt> of an integral @ref number "number" larger than its precision replaced its low
      digits with zeros, so the text did not convert back to the same number
    - the hour, minute and second of an absolute date whose UTC and local times are on opposite sides of the Unix
      epoch were negative (ex: <tt>1969-12-31T20:15:25-10:00</tt>)
    - the ISO week of the last days of a year with 53 ISO weeks that starts on a Thursday was reported as week 1 of
      the next year (ex: 2004-12-31 is <tt>2004-W53-5</tt>)
    - @ref Util::get_random_string() "get_random_string()" read \c /dev/urandom, so it raised
      \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED in a program that is not allowed to access the filesystem, and a
      @ref WebSocketHandler::WebSocketHandler "WebSocketHandler" could not be constructed in a sandboxed service; it
      now uses @ref Qore::get_random_bytes()
    - a destructor run while the @ref static_class_variables "static class variables" of a program were released
      (at process exit for a user module or the main program, or when a @ref Qore::Program "Program" was deleted)
      could crash the process if it created a Program: the new Program shares the program's public classes, and
      deleting it deleted their static variables while the program still used them
    - deleting @ref Qore::Program "Program" objects that share a class in different threads at the same time could
      crash the process
    - a @ref Qore::Program "Program" created while a program's data was released (by a destructor at exit, for
      example) shared the values of the program's static class variables, so storing it in one of them kept those
      values, and itself, alive; it now sees the values until they are released, and accessing them afterwards
      raises a \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception (see @ref static_class_variables)
    - <b>behavior change:</b> the values of the static variables of a public class are released when the
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" that defines it is deleted, even if programs that inherited the class still exist;
      accessing them from those programs then raises \c PROGRAM-ERROR, as calling the class's methods does (see
      @ref static_class_variables)
    - the constants and static class variables of a module loaded at runtime while a program's data was released
      (by a destructor at exit, for example) were never released
    - key-value data set with the C++ API function QoreClass::setKeyValue() was never released for a class without
      static variables
    - after a failed parse, a @ref Qore::Program "Program" without @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_REPARSE "PO_ALLOW_REPARSE"
      (which a child program does not get when its parent does not have it) lost the classes, constants and other
      symbols that it had inherited from its parent, so a new parse could not find them; only what the failed parse
      added is removed now
    - after a failed parse, a @ref Qore::Program "Program" with @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_REPARSE "PO_ALLOW_REPARSE" kept
      the classes, constants, hashdecls, enums and namespaces that the failed parse had declared, so a new parse could
      not declare them again
    - a function first referenced in a method was parse-initialized in the method's class, namespace and local
      variables, so its body and default argument values could call the class's private methods or read the
      method's locals, also when called from elsewhere; see @ref private_methods

    @section qore_2_2_1 Qore 2.2.1

    @par Release Summary
    Minor bugfix release including compatibility with GCC 16; see below for more information

    @subsection qore_2_2_1_bug_fixes Bug Fixes in Qore
    - @ref dataproviderintro "DataProvider" module
      - fixed the bulk record interface to allow implementations to return @ref nothing or an empty hash at the end
        of iteration
      - added the missing \c field and \c all entries to the expression role code map
    - @ref gmaildataproviderintro "GmailDataProvider" module
      - allow message event objects to be created when optional email headers are missing
    - @ref mewsrestdataproviderintro "MewsRestDataProvider" module
